WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:09.480
You know, usually when we talk about computer memory or even our own human memory, there's this expectation of a static, highly organized archiving system.

00:09.660 --> 00:10.620
Right, like a filing cabinet.

00:10.820 --> 00:25.800
Yeah, exactly. I always picture this massive, well-lit warehouse. You walk in, you put a box on a designated shelf, the inventory database logs the exact location, and then, you know, a year later when you need it, you just point at the shelf and say, there it is.

00:25.800 --> 00:32.800
It is an incredibly comforting visualization. I mean, we gravitate toward the idea of a localized state.

00:33.680 --> 00:34.420
The localized state.

00:34.440 --> 00:46.700
Yeah, a fixed snapshot of exactly how things are right now. We build our mental models and our physical machines, too, around the illusion that the current state of a system tells us everything we need to know about it.

00:46.720 --> 00:48.620
Like box on the shelf or no box on the shelf.

00:48.640 --> 00:48.780
Yeah.

00:48.980 --> 00:50.060
Binary. Clean.

00:50.380 --> 00:51.380
Exactly. Clean.

00:51.380 --> 00:59.640
But then you step into the world of possibility space and trajectory-based computation, and, well, suddenly that pristine warehouse just burns to the ground.

00:59.700 --> 01:00.800
Oh, absolutely ashes.

01:01.180 --> 01:09.280
We are looking at a computational and cognitive landscape today that is an absolute inversion of how we normally perceive reality.

01:10.120 --> 01:16.220
So, if you are joining us for this deep dive, you're likely looking for that intellectual aha moment, right?

01:16.220 --> 01:21.160
The one that bridges your everyday habits with some seriously deep theoretical computer science.

01:21.220 --> 01:27.400
Yes. And we have an incredibly dense, profoundly fascinating source to get us there today.

01:27.540 --> 01:39.300
We do. The document on the table is a 96-page monograph. It's titled Sphere Pop, A Complete Theory, Histories, Continuations, and the Algebra of Reachable Computation.

01:39.540 --> 01:40.380
That is quite the title.

01:40.380 --> 01:50.740
Right. It is written by an independent researcher who goes by the name Flyxion. And, um, on the surface, it functions as a complete formal theory for a radically new programming language called Sphere Pop.

01:50.860 --> 01:52.380
But it's not just a programming manual.

01:52.540 --> 02:03.860
Not at all. Beneath the syntax and all the theorems, it doubles as a sweeping philosophical treatise. It touches on epistemology, the nature of memory, and, like, the foundational infrastructure of human civilization.

02:03.860 --> 02:12.340
So, the mission of this deep dive is to completely dismantle how we think about computer programming. And, by extension, our own memories.

02:12.640 --> 02:13.380
That's the goal.

02:13.640 --> 02:27.160
We are going to explore a radical, almost disorienting claim. The claim that history is the fundamental substance. And, you know, the way things are right now, that box on the shelf, is nothing more than a mathematical illusion.

02:27.160 --> 02:35.880
We are going to fundamentally rethink what a simple, scribbled, grocery list has in common with the deepest mathematics of computation.

02:36.160 --> 02:40.300
I love that. The core convergence of the paper is stated very early on.

02:40.320 --> 02:40.540
Right.

02:40.580 --> 02:42.260
It sets the tone for everything.

02:42.580 --> 02:50.120
Yeah. The author writes something really striking. They say, a note is an externalized continuation. A Sphere Pop history is an internalized note.

02:50.120 --> 02:59.600
Okay. Let's unpack that. Because, to understand how computers should work, according to the Sphere Pop architecture, we actually have to step away from the keyboard first.

02:59.680 --> 03:04.000
We do. We have to look at how human beings externalize knowledge in the physical world.

03:04.080 --> 03:05.800
Right. We have to talk about taking notes.

03:06.120 --> 03:10.080
The paper starts by attacking something it calls the passive theory of notes.

03:10.480 --> 03:18.640
Yes. So, the passive theory of notes is the default assumption most of us operate under. It posits that notes are essentially prosthetic memories.

03:18.640 --> 03:20.660
Like a crutch for a leaky brain.

03:20.740 --> 03:30.460
Exactly. They are viewed as passive records of the past, created specifically to compensate for the biological reality that our brains are incredibly forgetful.

03:30.520 --> 03:30.740
Sure.

03:31.060 --> 03:36.500
So, under this theory, a note's entire value is judged by its retrospective fidelity.

03:37.340 --> 03:44.360
Retrospective fidelity, meaning, how accurately does this piece of paper reflect what just happened?

03:44.360 --> 03:55.800
Precisely that. If you experience an event, say, an important lecture, and you write down what the speaker said, the passive theory argues that a good note perfectly reproduces the past event.

03:55.960 --> 03:58.460
And a bad note distorts it, or leaves things out.

03:58.540 --> 04:01.860
Right. The orientation of the artifact is entirely backward-looking.

04:01.960 --> 04:03.380
Okay, but I have to push back here.

04:03.400 --> 04:03.600
Yeah.

04:03.800 --> 04:06.460
Because isn't that exactly what taking notes in a meeting actually is?

04:06.540 --> 04:07.220
Tell me how you mean.

04:07.220 --> 04:20.480
Well, if I am sitting in a boardroom, and my boss is listing off a dozen action items for the quarter, and I am desperately typing on my laptop so I don't forget what was just said, I am quite literally recording the past.

04:20.680 --> 04:22.100
It definitely feels that way.

04:22.160 --> 04:29.080
Right. My brain is going to forget those 12 items by lunch. My notes are the prosthetic memory of that meeting.

04:29.080 --> 04:36.720
The author acknowledges that this retrospective illusion is really powerful, but Flyxion dismantles it by asking us to shift our gaze.

04:36.900 --> 04:37.460
Shift it where?

04:37.820 --> 04:44.920
Away from celebrated historical records or meeting minutes, and instead look at the most common, mundane notes we create.

04:45.780 --> 04:47.640
Consider a standard grocery list.

04:47.760 --> 04:50.000
Okay. Milk, eggs, bread.

04:50.400 --> 04:53.840
Look at that list. Does it record anything that happened in the past?

04:53.900 --> 04:56.720
Well, no. I mean, I haven't bought the eggs yet. I haven't even gone to the store.

04:56.720 --> 05:06.260
Exactly. The items on that list do not exist in the form the list anticipates. They haven't been purchased. They haven't been placed in your cart. They haven't been carried into your kitchen.

05:06.860 --> 05:08.440
Oh, wow. I see what you're saying.

05:08.540 --> 05:13.300
The list is definitively not a memory. It is a structured record of a future act.

05:13.600 --> 05:18.860
That is a very weird way to think about a piece of paper on my fridge.

05:19.280 --> 05:25.700
The author provides a whole cascade of these examples. A blueprint describes a building that has not yet been constructed.

05:25.700 --> 05:29.500
A musical playlist is a record of a future act of listening.

05:30.320 --> 05:34.060
Source code itself records computations that have not yet been executed.

05:34.500 --> 05:37.100
A calendar entry records a commitment you haven't yet honored.

05:37.360 --> 05:41.900
None of these artifacts are memories in the passive retrospective sense.

05:42.060 --> 05:42.380
Exactly.

05:42.540 --> 05:47.380
So the passive theory gets the phenomenology of note-taking completely backwards.

05:48.180 --> 05:54.120
When I write down a to-do list, I am not preserving the past at all. I am literally constructing a future.

05:54.120 --> 05:59.600
Taking it a step further, the paper introduces a concept from theoretical computer science called a continuation.

05:59.960 --> 06:01.000
A continuation. Okay.

06:01.140 --> 06:06.440
If a state is a snapshot of right now, a continuation is the entire sequence of what comes next.

06:06.700 --> 06:10.060
It is the future execution that is enabled by a present configuration.

06:10.500 --> 06:13.980
And this leads to the paper's core thesis on human artifacts, right?

06:14.060 --> 06:15.400
The continuation account of notes.

06:15.400 --> 06:23.180
Yes. The formal definition given in the source is that a note is any artifact that increases the reachability of a continuation.

06:23.640 --> 06:31.920
Reachability. Meaning it makes a specific future trajectory more probable or significantly less costly to traverse.

06:32.180 --> 06:32.480
Right.

06:32.960 --> 06:39.240
To measure this, Flyxion introduces two mathematical concepts that completely reframe how we value information.

06:39.380 --> 06:39.700
Which are?

06:39.700 --> 06:42.420
Continuation volume and reachability leverage.

06:43.140 --> 06:48.380
Continuation volume refers to the sheer number of distinct futures a single note opens up.

06:48.600 --> 06:50.600
Okay. Volume makes sense. What about the leverage part?

06:50.920 --> 06:53.700
Reachability leverage is where the everyday practicality hits.

06:54.160 --> 07:01.980
Reachability leverage is the energetic or temporal cost you save by having the note versus the cost of figuring everything out from first principles without it.

07:02.260 --> 07:03.900
The leverage aspect makes perfect sense.

07:03.900 --> 07:09.880
Let's say I'm reading a massive, incredibly dense, 500-page textbook on organic chemistry.

07:10.140 --> 07:10.380
Okay.

07:10.620 --> 07:17.320
And I come across a specific formula and I spend 10 seconds folding over the corner of the page and hitting a paragraph with a yellow highlighter.

07:17.540 --> 07:19.340
Just a 10-second physical action.

07:19.640 --> 07:19.920
Right.

07:20.620 --> 07:25.560
The cost of creating that note, the physical act of dog, earing, and highlighting is negligible.

07:25.820 --> 07:26.460
10 seconds.

07:26.460 --> 07:39.120
But two years later, when I am in a lab and I desperately need that exact formula, that 10-second physical deformation of the paper saves me three hours of frantically flipping through 500 pages.

07:39.320 --> 07:42.040
That is an insane amount of reachability leverage.

07:42.380 --> 07:42.540
Yeah.

07:42.740 --> 07:45.660
And notice what gives that dog-eared page its value.

07:45.900 --> 07:50.060
Its worth has absolutely nothing to do with how well it remembers the past act of you reading the book.

07:50.140 --> 07:53.240
Right. Its entire value is derived from the future access it guarantees.

07:53.240 --> 07:57.140
Flyxion argues that notes are actually anti-collapse artifacts.

07:57.700 --> 07:59.900
Our biological memory is constantly collapsing.

08:00.200 --> 08:03.080
The universe's entropy is constantly collapsing possibilities.

08:03.540 --> 08:09.840
So notes are physical anchors we drop into reality to preserve access to futures that would otherwise be permanently lost.

08:09.960 --> 08:10.860
Beautifully said.

08:11.000 --> 08:13.080
Okay. This is a brilliant philosophical reframing.

08:13.420 --> 08:15.060
But here is the massive disconnect.

08:15.060 --> 08:24.260
If human beings organically build notes to preserve access to futures, why on earth do we build computers to only care about the present moment?

08:24.480 --> 08:28.160
And this bridges us perfectly into the critique of traditional computation.

08:28.560 --> 08:37.760
Right. Because if we look at the standard premise of programming, the way everyone is taught to code from day one, it's entirely state-centric.

08:37.760 --> 08:44.180
You open any introductory textbook on Python or C++, and the very first lesson is about variables.

08:44.880 --> 08:49.260
You are taught that a variable is a named location in memory that holds a value.

08:49.440 --> 08:53.820
You write x equals 5. Then on the next line, you write y equals x plus 1.

08:53.980 --> 08:59.000
It's the warehouse analogy again. I have a box labeled x, and I put a 5 inside it.

08:59.080 --> 09:04.740
Right. It feels intuitive because it mirrors how we naively interact with physical objects.

09:04.740 --> 09:08.680
A ball is red. Then we paint it blue. Its state changed.

09:09.060 --> 09:11.280
Traditional computation operates on this paradigm.

09:11.600 --> 09:17.400
A program starts in an initial state. A sequence of instructions mutates that state step by step.

09:17.500 --> 09:22.280
And whatever the final state of the machine happens to be when the program halts, well, that's your answer.

09:22.380 --> 09:25.160
But the author of SpherePop calls this the state illusion.

09:25.500 --> 09:26.480
The state illusion.

09:26.800 --> 09:30.960
Yes. And there's a specific foundational mathematical proposition here.

09:31.420 --> 09:33.280
Proposition 2.2 in the text.

09:33.280 --> 09:37.200
It claims that state is not a real physical property.

09:37.920 --> 09:41.960
State is actually a degenerate projection that collapses and erases history.

09:42.500 --> 09:43.960
Okay. We need to break down the math here.

09:44.260 --> 09:48.880
What exactly does it mean that state is a degenerate projection?

09:49.500 --> 09:56.780
In mathematics, a function or a projection is injective if every distinct input maps to a unique distinct output.

09:57.220 --> 09:58.040
Nothing is lost.

09:58.260 --> 09:59.760
Okay. So a one-to-one mapping.

09:59.760 --> 10:08.120
Exactly. A projection becomes non-injective or degenerate when it collapses multiple, completely different inputs into the exact same output.

10:08.320 --> 10:09.440
Can you give me a visual for that?

10:09.680 --> 10:10.780
Think of a shadow on a wall.

10:11.200 --> 10:14.980
If you shine a light on a cylinder from the top down, it casts a circular shadow.

10:15.480 --> 10:19.040
If you shine a light on a sphere, it also casts a circular shadow.

10:19.180 --> 10:19.560
Oh, wow.

10:19.560 --> 10:24.880
The 3D history of those objects is completely different, but the 2D state, the shadow, is identical.

10:25.060 --> 10:29.660
The projection to a lower dimension threw away the vital context of what the object actually is.

10:29.920 --> 10:34.600
So applied to computers, the history is the sequence of all events that have actually occurred in the system.

10:35.420 --> 10:38.780
But the computer's RAM, its memory, is finite.

10:38.780 --> 10:44.240
Right. The space of all possible histories in an active system is infinitely large.

10:44.800 --> 10:48.960
Every action, every millisecond, adds a new branch to the tree of history.

10:49.340 --> 10:55.960
But the space of observable states, the physical transistors holding bits in a computer's memory, is strictly finite.

10:56.160 --> 11:03.500
Therefore, any mathematical mapping from the infinite space of history to the finite space of memory must be non-injective.

11:03.800 --> 11:06.180
The system is forced to throw away the trajectory.

11:06.180 --> 11:10.640
It keeps the summary, but it permanently burns the evidence of how it arrived at that summary.

11:11.100 --> 11:13.420
That lost information is the degeneracy.

11:13.980 --> 11:15.280
Why does this actually matter, though?

11:15.340 --> 11:20.100
I mean, as a programmer, if I just want to know that X is 5, why do I care how it became 5?

11:20.260 --> 11:27.420
The paper argues that you care because this foundation of amnesia is the root cause of almost every catastrophic failure in modern software.

11:27.840 --> 11:28.900
Really? Like what?

11:29.200 --> 11:32.740
Think about the most notorious, difficult-to-track bugs in computer science.

11:33.340 --> 11:34.300
Race conditions.

11:34.300 --> 11:36.140
Use after free vulnerabilities.

11:36.600 --> 11:37.200
Okay, yeah.

11:37.560 --> 11:38.420
Those are a nightmare.

11:38.420 --> 11:42.700
They do not happen because the final state is mathematically wrong.

11:43.160 --> 11:50.540
They happen precisely because traditional languages conflate distinct histories into a single, indistinguishable state.

11:50.800 --> 11:52.680
Let me make sure I'm wrapping my head around this.

11:52.740 --> 11:53.960
Let's take a race condition.

11:54.140 --> 11:54.540
Go for it.

11:54.540 --> 12:05.600
So, I have a banking app, and two different processes, Thread A and Thread B, are trying to access the same bank account variable at the exact same millisecond.

12:05.680 --> 12:07.640
Right, a classic concurrency problem.

12:07.800 --> 12:09.280
Thread A wants to deposit money.

12:09.500 --> 12:11.080
Thread B wants to withdraw money.

12:11.540 --> 12:13.600
Because they overlap, they overwrite each other.

12:13.840 --> 12:17.620
When the dust settles, the final state of the account variable might be $100.

12:17.620 --> 12:23.540
But the computer literally has no idea if Thread A wrote that $100 or if Thread B wrote it.

12:23.620 --> 12:25.880
Or if it was some corrupted combination of both.

12:26.480 --> 12:32.840
The state says $100, but the history of who did what and when have been permanently erased by the architecture of the language.

12:33.080 --> 12:35.880
That is the exact mechanism of a race condition.

12:36.460 --> 12:41.720
Or consider a use-after-free bug, which is how a massive percentage of security hacks occur.

12:41.880 --> 12:42.980
How does that play out here?

12:42.980 --> 12:48.180
A program creates a pointer, a reference to a specific location in memory.

12:48.860 --> 12:52.160
Later, the program says, I'm done with this memory, free it up.

12:52.460 --> 12:53.440
The memory is freed.

12:53.860 --> 12:57.820
But due to a bug, the original pointer still exists in the system.

12:58.100 --> 12:58.580
Exactly.

12:59.000 --> 13:00.820
The state of that pointer hasn't changed.

13:00.940 --> 13:03.620
It still holds the exact same memory address.

13:03.840 --> 13:10.760
But the history of that memory region, the crucial fact that a free event occurred, is not attached to the pointer's state.

13:10.940 --> 13:12.260
The system forgot the trajectory.

13:12.260 --> 13:19.800
So the program follows the pointer, accesses memory it no longer owns, and the system crashes or gets hijacked by a malicious payload.

13:20.040 --> 13:24.400
I am thinking about how developers actually deal with this in the real world.

13:24.800 --> 13:27.780
And it is honestly wild when you frame it this way.

13:28.040 --> 13:32.000
It's the difference between a conventional software debugger and a git commit history.

13:32.140 --> 13:34.120
Oh, that is a fantastic comparison.

13:34.120 --> 13:43.140
If my Python code crashes and I open a traditional debugger, I am basically stepping into the role of a forensic archaeologist arriving at a crime scene.

13:43.400 --> 13:53.060
I am looking at a crash state, a dead body on the floor, and I am desperately trying to reverse engineer a timeline of events based on scattered clues.

13:53.060 --> 13:58.920
Because the actual evidence of the timeline was systematically destroyed by the computer as it was executing.

13:58.920 --> 14:02.700
A debugger is a tool designed to mitigate the tragedy of state erasure.

14:03.240 --> 14:07.560
You are trying to guess the history that the machine was explicitly designed to forget.

14:08.080 --> 14:12.060
But if I look at a version control system like Git, the philosophy is completely different.

14:12.900 --> 14:16.300
A Git repository isn't just the current file tree sitting on my hard drive.

14:16.300 --> 14:19.820
No, Git proves that the history is the actual substance.

14:20.120 --> 14:22.800
And the current file tree is just a temporary, degenerate summary.

14:23.720 --> 14:26.520
In Git, I don't have to guess how a bug got introduced.

14:27.220 --> 14:30.820
I could see every single branch, every line of code added or deleted,

14:31.180 --> 14:36.740
every commit with a timestamp and an author stretching all the way back to the very first day the project started.

14:36.740 --> 14:40.700
And this is the exact revelation that birthed the SpherePop architecture.

14:40.700 --> 14:46.080
If history is what gets lost in traditional computing, causing structural fragility,

14:46.400 --> 14:49.840
then history must become the primary substance of the machine.

14:50.340 --> 14:53.120
SpherePop refuses to discard the trajectory.

14:53.480 --> 14:59.180
The author points out that the state illusion in computing and the passive theory of notes in human cognition

14:59.180 --> 15:02.380
are actually the exact same philosophical mistake.

15:02.460 --> 15:02.860
How so?

15:02.860 --> 15:08.260
Both identify value with a terminal state, the final biological memory,

15:08.500 --> 15:12.940
or the final variable value rather than respecting the trajectory that produced it.

15:12.940 --> 15:15.080
Okay, I am fully bought into the problem.

15:15.200 --> 15:15.440
Yes.

15:15.600 --> 15:18.840
State is an illusion that causes amnesia and crashes.

15:19.640 --> 15:21.320
History is the truth.

15:21.620 --> 15:22.420
Glad you're on board.

15:22.420 --> 15:27.880
So practically speaking, how do you actually build a functioning programming language out of history?

15:28.240 --> 15:32.620
I mean, if I can't use variables, if I can't just say, store this number here, what am I even doing?

15:32.620 --> 15:37.420
This brings us to the theoretical core of the paper, the four operators of possibility.

15:37.580 --> 15:38.240
The four operators.

15:38.400 --> 15:38.600
Okay.

15:38.840 --> 15:42.780
If you strip away the concept of a variable and you strip away the concept of a state,

15:42.980 --> 15:45.100
what is the fundamental fabric you are left with?

15:46.020 --> 15:49.140
According to SpherePop, you are lumped with possibility space,

15:49.600 --> 15:51.940
the infinite landscape of all potential futures.

15:52.620 --> 15:55.940
The author proposes that you do not need variables or memory addresses.

15:55.940 --> 16:00.820
You only need a minimal, mathematically complete set of four operations

16:00.820 --> 16:03.940
to navigate and shape this possibility space.

16:04.560 --> 16:09.620
And these four operations are entirely sufficient to build any conceivable computer program.

16:09.840 --> 16:15.400
The four operators are pop, refuse, bind, and collapse.

16:15.580 --> 16:17.200
We need to go through these meticulously.

16:17.540 --> 16:18.380
Let's start with pop.

16:18.660 --> 16:20.100
What is the pop operator?

16:20.280 --> 16:21.960
Pop is the operator of commitment.

16:22.620 --> 16:24.720
Its function is to narrow possibility.

16:24.720 --> 16:26.060
Like making a choice.

16:26.260 --> 16:26.580
Exactly.

16:27.000 --> 16:30.240
Imagine you are standing at a crossroads with five distinct paths.

16:30.940 --> 16:34.660
Pop is the singular act of committing to one specific path

16:34.660 --> 16:37.620
and irreversibly removing it from the space of options.

16:37.820 --> 16:39.700
It is the primitive act of moving forward in time.

16:39.800 --> 16:40.060
Yes.

16:40.300 --> 16:42.080
When you pop a value or an action,

16:42.280 --> 16:44.980
you are effectively foreclosing all other alternate futures

16:44.980 --> 16:46.320
in which your choice was different.

16:46.460 --> 16:47.760
So pop is taking a step.

16:47.980 --> 16:49.160
It's executing a choice.

16:49.280 --> 16:49.600
Got it.

16:49.740 --> 16:51.000
The second operator is refuse.

16:51.360 --> 16:53.960
The paper calls this documented inadmissibility.

16:53.960 --> 16:57.300
This operator is arguably the most crucial invention in the architecture.

16:58.060 --> 17:01.740
Refuse is how the system records that a specific path in the possibility space

17:01.740 --> 17:03.720
was found to be invalid or dangerous.

17:04.160 --> 17:07.560
But, and this is the vital distinction from traditional error handling,

17:08.560 --> 17:11.640
the refuse operator always requires a reason, right?

17:11.900 --> 17:12.220
Always.

17:12.400 --> 17:14.820
It doesn't just block a path with a generic no.

17:15.140 --> 17:18.640
It requires a formal proof of no because of X.

17:18.840 --> 17:19.120
Okay.

17:19.120 --> 17:22.880
Furthermore, unlike pop, which consumes a choice,

17:23.460 --> 17:26.340
refuse does not remove the option from the structural space.

17:26.980 --> 17:28.360
It leaves the path visible,

17:28.760 --> 17:32.880
but marks it as semantically inadmissible for any future trajectory.

17:33.220 --> 17:36.240
I want to really ground why the reason is so important.

17:36.540 --> 17:40.060
Why can't the system just hit a dead end, throw a red flag, and bounce back?

17:40.160 --> 17:42.700
Because without a permanent attached reason,

17:42.960 --> 17:46.240
a refused path is completely indistinguishable from a path

17:46.240 --> 17:48.640
that was simply never considered in the first place.

17:48.760 --> 17:48.940
Ah.

17:49.120 --> 17:51.380
The reason is the evidence of the exploration.

17:51.680 --> 17:54.820
The reason becomes a first-class component of the system's history.

17:55.280 --> 17:56.120
Let me try an analogy.

17:56.680 --> 17:58.380
Let's say we were exploring a labyrinth.

17:58.820 --> 18:00.600
If I walk down a corridor and simply tell you,

18:00.920 --> 18:02.340
don't open the blue door at the end,

18:02.460 --> 18:03.380
you might listen to me today.

18:03.480 --> 18:03.700
Sure.

18:03.900 --> 18:04.680
But a week later,

18:05.120 --> 18:06.980
when you are stressed and looking for an exit,

18:07.100 --> 18:07.540
you might think,

18:07.600 --> 18:09.160
well, maybe he just didn't like the color blue,

18:09.200 --> 18:10.020
and you open it anyway.

18:10.320 --> 18:11.020
Human nature.

18:11.020 --> 18:12.880
But if I use the refuse operator,

18:13.460 --> 18:14.360
I am saying,

18:14.760 --> 18:15.900
don't open the blue door,

18:16.300 --> 18:18.220
and here is the irrefutable proof

18:18.220 --> 18:20.460
that there is a literal tiger behind it.

18:20.580 --> 18:21.520
That changes things.

18:21.680 --> 18:21.840
Right.

18:22.120 --> 18:25.220
That attached reason fundamentally and permanently

18:25.220 --> 18:27.760
alters your future relationship with that door.

18:28.400 --> 18:29.880
It's not just a blocked path.

18:30.200 --> 18:31.940
It is documented knowledge.

18:32.240 --> 18:33.920
A perfect translation of the concept.

18:34.500 --> 18:36.800
The third operator in the set is bind.

18:36.980 --> 18:40.720
The paper defines this as dependency declaration.

18:41.020 --> 18:42.140
What are we binding together?

18:42.420 --> 18:45.820
Bind couples two independent trajectory elements together

18:45.820 --> 18:47.560
without consuming either of them.

18:47.720 --> 18:48.980
It records a relationship.

18:49.240 --> 18:49.500
Okay.

18:49.500 --> 18:51.040
If pop is taking a step

18:51.040 --> 18:52.940
and refuse is documenting a tiger,

18:53.640 --> 18:55.280
bind is drawing a line on a map

18:55.280 --> 18:56.700
connecting your current location

18:56.700 --> 18:58.020
to the location of the tiger.

18:58.640 --> 18:58.960
It says,

18:59.240 --> 19:00.340
the existence of this element

19:00.340 --> 19:01.700
is now formally dependent

19:01.700 --> 19:03.160
on the existence of that element.

19:03.540 --> 19:05.080
And finally, we have the fourth operator.

19:05.200 --> 19:05.340
Go.

19:05.420 --> 19:05.860
Collapse.

19:06.300 --> 19:08.280
The paper calls this observation under rule.

19:08.720 --> 19:10.420
Collapse is essentially a meta operation.

19:10.420 --> 19:12.340
Pop, refuse, and bind

19:12.340 --> 19:13.840
all operate silently

19:13.840 --> 19:14.640
on the invisible,

19:14.900 --> 19:16.600
ever-growing history of the system.

19:16.760 --> 19:18.200
But we eventually need to see something.

19:18.560 --> 19:18.800
Right.

19:19.180 --> 19:20.200
Collapse is the mechanism

19:20.200 --> 19:22.060
that makes those invisible histories

19:22.060 --> 19:23.160
visible to an observer.

19:23.840 --> 19:26.580
It maps the vast, complex history space

19:26.580 --> 19:29.060
down to a finite, observable state.

19:29.180 --> 19:30.200
But it does so safely.

19:30.560 --> 19:31.460
It acts as a lens.

19:31.840 --> 19:32.740
And, crucially,

19:33.140 --> 19:34.740
a collapse operation requires

19:34.740 --> 19:36.200
an admissibility certificate.

19:36.200 --> 19:37.940
A proof that the history

19:37.940 --> 19:38.780
it is summarizing

19:38.780 --> 19:39.780
is actually valid

19:39.780 --> 19:41.200
and hasn't violated

19:41.200 --> 19:42.620
any refuse documents

19:42.620 --> 19:43.240
along the way.

19:43.700 --> 19:44.480
I have to pause here.

19:44.700 --> 19:44.880
I mean,

19:45.000 --> 19:46.020
I have written

19:46.020 --> 19:47.160
my fair share of code.

19:47.700 --> 19:48.140
Python,

19:48.580 --> 19:48.960
JavaScript,

19:49.300 --> 19:49.860
a little C.

19:50.360 --> 19:51.160
So you know

19:51.160 --> 19:52.040
the standard toolkit.

19:52.280 --> 19:52.480
Yeah.

19:52.580 --> 19:53.520
And I am looking at these

19:53.520 --> 19:55.640
four highly abstract,

19:56.040 --> 19:57.520
almost philosophical verbs.

19:57.720 --> 19:58.060
Commit,

19:58.420 --> 19:58.880
refuse,

19:59.260 --> 19:59.600
bind,

19:59.980 --> 20:00.300
observe.

20:00.540 --> 20:01.740
Are you seriously telling me

20:01.740 --> 20:02.440
that I can build

20:02.440 --> 20:04.300
any standard computer program

20:04.300 --> 20:05.060
with just this?

20:05.400 --> 20:05.800
I am.

20:05.900 --> 20:06.920
I can build an if statement.

20:07.100 --> 20:07.960
I can manage RAM.

20:08.200 --> 20:09.640
I can handle network concurrency

20:09.640 --> 20:11.340
with just these four things.

20:11.460 --> 20:12.240
The paper dedicates

20:12.240 --> 20:13.380
a massive section,

20:13.660 --> 20:14.600
theorem 3.7,

20:14.660 --> 20:15.180
the completeness

20:15.180 --> 20:16.000
of the four operators,

20:16.140 --> 20:17.200
to proving exactly that.

20:17.540 --> 20:18.360
Everything you do

20:18.360 --> 20:19.340
in a traditional language

20:19.340 --> 20:20.140
is just a macro

20:20.140 --> 20:20.900
built on top

20:20.900 --> 20:21.760
of these four primitives.

20:21.860 --> 20:22.540
Okay, prove it.

20:22.620 --> 20:23.820
Let's take the most basic example

20:23.820 --> 20:24.500
I brought up earlier.

20:24.840 --> 20:26.500
Variable assignment dot x equals 5.

20:26.820 --> 20:27.060
Right.

20:27.320 --> 20:29.040
How do you do x equals 5

20:29.040 --> 20:30.000
without a variable?

20:30.260 --> 20:30.460
Yeah.

20:30.640 --> 20:31.480
In SpherePop,

20:31.660 --> 20:33.120
variable assignment doesn't exist.

20:33.620 --> 20:33.920
Instead,

20:34.380 --> 20:35.360
the system executes

20:35.360 --> 20:36.820
a pop of the value 5,

20:37.080 --> 20:38.100
committing the number 5

20:38.100 --> 20:38.860
into the history.

20:39.060 --> 20:39.760
Okay, so the 5

20:39.760 --> 20:40.520
is a step we took.

20:40.620 --> 20:40.920
Then,

20:41.040 --> 20:42.040
it executes a bind,

20:42.220 --> 20:44.280
coupling the human-readable symbol x

20:44.280 --> 20:46.520
to that specific historical pop event.

20:46.960 --> 20:48.620
The value isn't stored in x.

20:49.060 --> 20:50.340
x is just a pointer

20:50.340 --> 20:51.580
to a moment in history

20:51.580 --> 20:52.700
where 5 was chosen.

20:52.940 --> 20:53.240
Wow.

20:53.400 --> 20:53.600
Okay.

20:54.020 --> 20:55.000
What about control flow?

20:55.400 --> 20:56.720
How do I write an if statement?

20:56.880 --> 20:57.100
Mm-hmm.

20:57.100 --> 20:58.280
Like, if the weather is raining,

20:58.640 --> 20:59.180
take an umbrella.

20:59.580 --> 20:59.820
Otherwise,

21:00.220 --> 21:00.920
take sunglasses.

21:00.920 --> 21:02.120
An if statement

21:02.120 --> 21:03.240
is a bifurcation

21:03.240 --> 21:04.700
of possibility space.

21:05.200 --> 21:06.100
The system evaluates

21:06.100 --> 21:06.600
the condition.

21:07.000 --> 21:07.520
Is it raining?

21:08.140 --> 21:09.540
That evaluation is a pop.

21:09.840 --> 21:11.040
Let's say it is raining.

21:11.180 --> 21:11.380
Then,

21:11.500 --> 21:12.240
the system pops

21:12.240 --> 21:13.120
the true branch

21:13.120 --> 21:14.140
taking the umbrella.

21:14.360 --> 21:14.940
But what about

21:14.940 --> 21:15.880
the false branch?

21:16.120 --> 21:17.080
In traditional code,

21:17.160 --> 21:17.780
the false branch

21:17.780 --> 21:18.820
is just ignored.

21:19.260 --> 21:19.900
In SpherePop,

21:20.000 --> 21:20.820
the system uses

21:20.820 --> 21:21.960
the refuse operator

21:21.960 --> 21:22.920
on the false branch,

21:23.140 --> 21:24.060
permanently documenting

21:24.060 --> 21:25.000
exactly why

21:25.000 --> 21:26.180
the sunglasses trajectory

21:26.180 --> 21:27.080
was not taken,

21:27.080 --> 21:28.520
because the rain condition

21:28.520 --> 21:29.820
evaluated to true.

21:30.180 --> 21:30.720
So the history

21:30.720 --> 21:32.340
explicitly records

21:32.340 --> 21:33.520
both the path taken

21:33.520 --> 21:34.580
and the mathematically

21:34.580 --> 21:35.580
certified reason

21:35.580 --> 21:36.460
the alternate path

21:36.460 --> 21:37.020
was rejected.

21:37.380 --> 21:37.860
Exactly.

21:38.020 --> 21:38.900
That is mind-blowing.

21:39.220 --> 21:40.240
The code doesn't just

21:40.240 --> 21:41.180
know what it did,

21:41.240 --> 21:42.460
it knows exactly why

21:42.460 --> 21:43.660
it didn't do something else.

21:43.760 --> 21:43.960
Right.

21:44.120 --> 21:45.360
What about when things go wrong?

21:45.420 --> 21:46.160
What about exceptions

21:46.160 --> 21:47.360
or system crashes?

21:47.700 --> 21:48.920
An exception being thrown

21:48.920 --> 21:49.700
is no longer

21:49.700 --> 21:50.680
a catastrophic event

21:50.680 --> 21:52.020
that destroys the call stack.

21:52.480 --> 21:53.480
An exception is simply

21:53.480 --> 21:54.500
a refuse operator

21:54.500 --> 21:55.640
that carries its reason,

21:55.640 --> 21:57.580
perhaps a file-not-found error

21:57.580 --> 21:58.880
as a first-class certificate.

21:59.240 --> 22:00.220
The system doesn't crash.

22:00.740 --> 22:01.520
It just adds

22:01.520 --> 22:03.120
a highly detailed refusal

22:03.120 --> 22:03.900
to the history.

22:04.160 --> 22:04.480
Yes.

22:04.960 --> 22:05.880
And memory management,

22:06.760 --> 22:07.480
freeing up RAM.

22:07.880 --> 22:09.100
Even memory deallocation

22:09.100 --> 22:10.440
is just a refuse operator

22:10.440 --> 22:11.700
applied to a memory pointer.

22:12.140 --> 22:13.820
It records that the memory region

22:13.820 --> 22:15.040
is no longer admissible

22:15.040 --> 22:15.580
to access.

22:15.900 --> 22:16.920
So if a later part

22:16.920 --> 22:17.420
of the program

22:17.420 --> 22:18.820
tries to access that memory?

22:18.900 --> 22:19.580
Which would cause

22:19.580 --> 22:20.900
a use-after-free bug

22:20.900 --> 22:21.540
in C++.

22:21.960 --> 22:22.160
Right.

22:22.300 --> 22:23.440
SpherePop simply traces

22:23.440 --> 22:24.020
the history,

22:24.560 --> 22:25.320
hits the refuse

22:25.320 --> 22:25.860
documentation,

22:26.520 --> 22:27.060
and stops.

22:27.580 --> 22:28.840
It turns a silent,

22:29.080 --> 22:30.200
dangerous memory corruption

22:30.200 --> 22:31.140
into a clean,

22:31.520 --> 22:32.460
mathematically proven

22:32.460 --> 22:33.460
type-level error.

22:33.960 --> 22:34.900
The history protects

22:34.900 --> 22:35.400
the future.

22:35.840 --> 22:36.820
The elegance of that

22:36.820 --> 22:37.800
is hard to overstate.

22:38.680 --> 22:39.920
And out of this architecture,

22:40.360 --> 22:41.040
Fitchin derives

22:41.040 --> 22:41.740
this overarching

22:41.740 --> 22:42.880
mathematical principle

22:42.880 --> 22:44.220
called the conservation

22:44.220 --> 22:45.600
law of possibility.

22:45.980 --> 22:47.140
Theorem 3.8.

22:47.320 --> 22:48.300
It reads like a law

22:48.300 --> 22:49.100
of thermodynamics

22:49.100 --> 22:49.900
for computation.

22:49.900 --> 22:51.400
It mathematically proves

22:51.400 --> 22:52.620
that in this architecture,

22:53.120 --> 22:54.640
possibility cannot be created

22:54.640 --> 22:55.240
or destroyed.

22:55.740 --> 22:56.900
It can only be consumed

22:56.900 --> 22:57.620
by a pop

22:57.620 --> 22:58.580
or documented

22:58.580 --> 22:59.360
by a refuse.

22:59.660 --> 23:01.140
Because every single operation,

23:01.420 --> 23:02.120
whether it succeeds

23:02.120 --> 23:02.700
or fails,

23:03.000 --> 23:03.860
must be appended

23:03.860 --> 23:04.500
to the log.

23:04.980 --> 23:05.820
Every operation

23:05.820 --> 23:07.180
strictly increases

23:07.180 --> 23:08.080
the overall length

23:08.080 --> 23:09.300
of the system's history.

23:09.520 --> 23:10.640
Which means time

23:10.640 --> 23:11.700
in the SpherePop universe

23:11.700 --> 23:13.140
is strictly irreversible.

23:13.700 --> 23:15.420
You can never truly hit undo

23:15.420 --> 23:17.120
and return to a pristine

23:17.120 --> 23:18.060
prior state.

23:18.180 --> 23:19.140
Because to go backwards,

23:19.560 --> 23:20.920
to truly erase a mistake

23:20.920 --> 23:22.220
would require erasing

23:22.220 --> 23:23.460
the history of the steps

23:23.460 --> 23:24.700
you took to make the mistake,

23:24.900 --> 23:25.620
which violates

23:25.620 --> 23:26.760
the foundational premise

23:26.760 --> 23:28.100
of the entire framework.

23:28.380 --> 23:29.120
In SpherePop,

23:29.300 --> 23:30.820
computation isn't the manipulation

23:30.820 --> 23:31.980
of static values.

23:32.800 --> 23:34.160
Computation is the progressive,

23:34.660 --> 23:35.820
irreversible restriction

23:35.820 --> 23:36.600
of possibility.

23:36.740 --> 23:37.440
Beautifully put.

23:37.600 --> 23:38.380
This is where the paper

23:38.380 --> 23:39.240
pulls off a maneuver

23:39.240 --> 23:40.680
that completely caught me off guard.

23:40.720 --> 23:40.860
Yeah.

23:40.940 --> 23:41.680
The taxonomy.

23:42.000 --> 23:42.320
Yes.

23:43.000 --> 23:44.400
Having spent 40 pages

23:44.400 --> 23:45.300
establishing these

23:45.300 --> 23:46.640
four mathematical operators,

23:46.940 --> 23:48.180
pop, refuse, bind,

23:48.320 --> 23:49.780
collapse for machine computation,

23:49.780 --> 23:51.920
Flyxion suddenly pans

23:51.920 --> 23:52.840
the camera back out

23:52.840 --> 23:53.900
to human civilization.

23:54.540 --> 23:55.420
The author maps

23:55.420 --> 23:56.860
these exact same

23:56.860 --> 23:58.060
four operators

23:58.060 --> 23:59.720
onto the physical artifacts

23:59.720 --> 24:01.200
we create in society,

24:01.780 --> 24:02.840
generating what is called

24:02.840 --> 24:04.780
the SpherePop note taxonomy.

24:05.120 --> 24:06.500
It is a stunning convergence.

24:07.400 --> 24:08.160
The assertion is that

24:08.160 --> 24:09.040
the artifacts we use

24:09.040 --> 24:10.320
to organize human society

24:10.320 --> 24:11.600
aren't just loosely

24:11.600 --> 24:12.940
analogous to computation.

24:13.360 --> 24:14.700
They are literal realizations

24:14.700 --> 24:15.760
of the exact same

24:15.760 --> 24:16.700
four operators.

24:17.200 --> 24:18.240
The paper identifies

24:18.240 --> 24:20.040
seven distinct classes of notes.

24:20.120 --> 24:21.140
Let's weave through these

24:21.140 --> 24:22.320
because it completely changes

24:22.320 --> 24:23.140
how you look at a desk

24:23.140 --> 24:23.680
full of paper.

24:24.060 --> 24:25.420
The first class is capture notes.

24:25.740 --> 24:26.540
Capture notes map

24:26.540 --> 24:28.120
directly to the refuse operator.

24:28.460 --> 24:29.320
The primary examples

24:29.320 --> 24:30.180
are photographs,

24:30.360 --> 24:31.000
audio recordings,

24:31.180 --> 24:32.060
or a scientist's

24:32.060 --> 24:33.200
raw laboratory notebook.

24:33.320 --> 24:33.920
What are they doing?

24:34.000 --> 24:35.200
They are refusing the collapse

24:35.200 --> 24:36.420
of a perceptual trajectory.

24:37.100 --> 24:38.260
When you take a photograph

24:38.260 --> 24:39.060
of a sunset,

24:39.480 --> 24:40.140
that photograph

24:40.140 --> 24:41.560
is a physical artifact

24:41.560 --> 24:42.080
asserting,

24:42.720 --> 24:43.540
I refuse to let

24:43.540 --> 24:44.940
the infinite visual complexity

24:44.940 --> 24:45.960
of the specific moment

24:45.960 --> 24:46.940
in history be collapsed

24:46.940 --> 24:47.780
into a vague,

24:48.140 --> 24:48.940
low-resolution

24:48.940 --> 24:50.040
biological memory.

24:50.400 --> 24:51.020
They are anchors

24:51.020 --> 24:51.820
against the decay

24:51.820 --> 24:52.480
of perception.

24:52.820 --> 24:53.360
I love that.

24:54.240 --> 24:55.360
The second class

24:55.360 --> 24:56.600
is perspective notes,

24:56.820 --> 24:57.340
which maps

24:57.340 --> 24:58.460
to the bind operator.

24:58.620 --> 24:59.680
We touched on these earlier.

24:59.960 --> 25:00.480
Calendars,

25:00.680 --> 25:01.760
architectural blueprints,

25:01.940 --> 25:02.860
project roadmaps,

25:03.020 --> 25:05.020
they bind a present state

25:05.020 --> 25:06.020
to a future action.

25:06.220 --> 25:06.740
Importantly,

25:06.860 --> 25:07.740
a calendar appointment

25:07.740 --> 25:08.780
does not execute

25:08.780 --> 25:09.280
the meeting.

25:09.440 --> 25:09.680
Right.

25:09.820 --> 25:10.580
That execution

25:10.580 --> 25:11.340
would be a POC.

25:11.800 --> 25:12.360
The calendar

25:12.360 --> 25:13.200
merely records

25:13.200 --> 25:13.900
the dependency

25:13.900 --> 25:14.920
between today

25:14.920 --> 25:15.540
and tomorrow.

25:15.540 --> 25:17.480
The paper points out

25:17.480 --> 25:18.240
that human-written

25:18.240 --> 25:18.820
source code

25:18.820 --> 25:19.360
is actually

25:19.360 --> 25:20.300
a perspective note.

25:20.820 --> 25:21.440
It binds

25:21.440 --> 25:22.900
a future machine execution

25:22.900 --> 25:23.740
to a present

25:23.740 --> 25:24.880
human specification.

25:25.500 --> 25:26.080
The third class

25:26.080 --> 25:27.220
is repair notes.

25:27.840 --> 25:28.460
These combine

25:28.460 --> 25:30.020
refuse and pop.

25:30.480 --> 25:31.340
Troubleshooting guides

25:31.340 --> 25:32.340
and maintenance manuals

25:32.340 --> 25:33.320
are the best examples.

25:33.980 --> 25:34.600
A repair note

25:34.600 --> 25:35.760
is a preemptive reduction

25:35.760 --> 25:37.200
of reconstruction costs.

25:37.280 --> 25:38.020
So it anticipates

25:38.020 --> 25:38.440
a failure.

25:38.600 --> 25:39.160
A refusal

25:39.160 --> 25:40.200
in the real world,

25:40.340 --> 25:41.300
like a printer jamming.

25:41.680 --> 25:42.520
It acknowledges

25:42.520 --> 25:43.100
that the system

25:43.100 --> 25:44.660
has hit an inadmissible state

25:44.660 --> 25:45.300
and it provides

25:45.300 --> 25:45.900
a stored,

25:46.100 --> 25:46.860
certified sequence

25:46.860 --> 25:47.460
of POPs.

25:47.820 --> 25:48.320
Step one,

25:48.580 --> 25:49.100
open tray.

25:49.500 --> 25:49.940
Step two,

25:50.260 --> 25:50.880
remove paper.

25:51.360 --> 25:52.220
This navigates

25:52.220 --> 25:52.800
the user back

25:52.800 --> 25:54.020
to a functional trajectory.

25:54.360 --> 25:54.780
The fourth

25:54.780 --> 25:55.780
is refusal notes.

25:56.080 --> 25:56.660
This is the pure

25:56.660 --> 25:57.140
application

25:57.140 --> 25:58.260
of the refuse operator.

25:58.700 --> 25:59.800
Mathematical proofs,

26:00.100 --> 26:01.180
cryptographic checksums,

26:01.640 --> 26:02.300
legal deeds,

26:02.660 --> 26:03.880
their entire function

26:03.880 --> 26:04.580
is to prevent

26:04.580 --> 26:05.220
the collapse

26:05.220 --> 26:05.940
of a distinction.

26:06.280 --> 26:07.200
Like a math proof.

26:07.360 --> 26:07.700
Yes.

26:07.700 --> 26:08.920
A 50-page

26:08.920 --> 26:09.920
mathematical proof

26:09.920 --> 26:11.180
refuses the collapse

26:11.180 --> 26:11.980
of a complex

26:11.980 --> 26:13.080
logical trajectory

26:13.080 --> 26:14.960
into a mere assertion.

26:15.480 --> 26:16.580
You don't have to just trust

26:16.580 --> 26:17.580
that a theorem is true.

26:18.260 --> 26:19.180
The refusal note

26:19.180 --> 26:20.540
preserves the exact

26:20.540 --> 26:21.780
rigorous path

26:21.780 --> 26:22.340
of truth,

26:22.660 --> 26:23.520
preventing anyone

26:23.520 --> 26:24.060
from taking

26:24.060 --> 26:24.960
an inadmissible

26:24.960 --> 26:25.960
logical shortcut

26:25.960 --> 26:27.080
whether or not

26:27.080 --> 26:27.980
anyone ever reads

26:27.980 --> 26:28.940
the full proof again.

26:29.220 --> 26:29.580
Fifth,

26:29.980 --> 26:31.160
we have generative notes.

26:31.560 --> 26:32.220
These correspond

26:32.220 --> 26:33.300
to the POP operator.

26:33.580 --> 26:34.300
Physical maps,

26:34.640 --> 26:35.440
scientific theories,

26:35.600 --> 26:36.280
grammar rules,

26:36.420 --> 26:37.300
they open paths

26:37.300 --> 26:38.060
that were previously

26:38.060 --> 26:39.200
completely unreachable.

26:39.280 --> 26:40.480
A map doesn't just record

26:40.480 --> 26:41.220
where someone walked?

26:41.300 --> 26:41.580
No,

26:41.580 --> 26:42.440
it creates access

26:42.440 --> 26:43.680
to entirely new

26:43.680 --> 26:44.380
continuations

26:44.380 --> 26:45.540
in physical possibility

26:45.540 --> 26:46.540
space for anyone

26:46.540 --> 26:47.260
holding them out.

26:47.600 --> 26:47.640
Okay,

26:47.780 --> 26:48.820
now for the sixth class.

26:49.180 --> 26:49.740
This is the one

26:49.740 --> 26:50.600
I really wrestled with.

26:51.020 --> 26:51.660
Collapse notes.

26:52.140 --> 26:52.960
Mapping directly

26:52.960 --> 26:54.000
to the collapse operator.

26:54.160 --> 26:54.760
The examples

26:54.760 --> 26:55.880
Flyxion gives are

26:55.880 --> 26:56.820
indexes in the back

26:56.820 --> 26:57.260
of a book,

26:57.660 --> 26:58.900
file names on a computer,

26:59.260 --> 27:00.560
biological taxonomies,

27:00.680 --> 27:01.880
and even human words

27:01.880 --> 27:02.400
themselves.

27:02.700 --> 27:03.420
This brings us

27:03.420 --> 27:04.560
to the profound paradox

27:04.560 --> 27:05.440
of the collapse note.

27:06.420 --> 27:07.440
Every other class

27:07.440 --> 27:08.540
of note we just discussed

27:08.540 --> 27:09.140
is designed

27:09.140 --> 27:10.320
to preserve information,

27:10.580 --> 27:11.380
to preserve history,

27:11.380 --> 27:11.780
Right.

27:12.040 --> 27:12.640
Collapse notes,

27:12.920 --> 27:13.940
mathematically speaking,

27:14.480 --> 27:15.740
exist to destroy

27:15.740 --> 27:16.420
information.

27:17.300 --> 27:18.560
They take a complex,

27:18.840 --> 27:19.500
rich history

27:19.500 --> 27:21.300
and forcefully compress it

27:21.300 --> 27:22.060
into a tiny,

27:22.200 --> 27:23.220
degenerate representation.

27:23.780 --> 27:24.600
Let's really dig

27:24.600 --> 27:25.420
into this paradox

27:25.420 --> 27:26.580
because the idea

27:26.580 --> 27:27.780
that destroying information

27:27.780 --> 27:29.580
is a necessary social good

27:29.580 --> 27:31.180
is counterintuitive.

27:31.400 --> 27:32.020
Very much so.

27:32.160 --> 27:32.360
Right.

27:32.400 --> 27:33.040
Let's say I write

27:33.040 --> 27:34.240
a 500-page biography

27:34.240 --> 27:35.140
of Abraham Lincoln.

27:35.260 --> 27:35.500
Okay.

27:35.660 --> 27:36.280
And then I give it

27:36.280 --> 27:36.980
the title Lincoln.

27:37.160 --> 27:37.400
Okay.

27:37.400 --> 27:38.560
By summarizing

27:38.560 --> 27:39.680
half a million words

27:39.680 --> 27:40.280
into a single

27:40.280 --> 27:41.160
seven-letter word,

27:41.500 --> 27:42.200
I have engaged

27:42.200 --> 27:43.420
in a massive destruction

27:43.420 --> 27:44.080
of information.

27:44.320 --> 27:45.120
I have thrown away

27:45.120 --> 27:47.380
99.99% of the nuance.

27:47.480 --> 27:48.640
But what Flyxion points out

27:48.640 --> 27:49.640
is that this violence,

27:49.800 --> 27:50.360
this destruction,

27:50.360 --> 27:51.820
is absolutely necessary

27:51.820 --> 27:52.900
to make the book reachable.

27:52.960 --> 27:53.300
Exactly.

27:53.460 --> 27:54.320
Let me phrase it differently.

27:54.720 --> 27:56.120
The author formalizes this

27:56.120 --> 27:57.440
in Theorem 13.2,

27:57.940 --> 27:58.500
the necessity

27:58.500 --> 27:59.420
of collapse notes.

28:00.100 --> 28:01.340
Imagine a massive

28:01.340 --> 28:02.340
national library

28:02.340 --> 28:03.980
containing 10 million books.

28:03.980 --> 28:04.360
Okay.

28:04.660 --> 28:05.440
But none of the books

28:05.440 --> 28:06.520
have titles on the spine.

28:06.920 --> 28:07.460
None of them have

28:07.460 --> 28:08.220
authors listed.

28:08.680 --> 28:10.320
And there is no card catalog.

28:10.840 --> 28:11.580
Sounds like a nightmare.

28:11.820 --> 28:13.020
The library preserves

28:13.020 --> 28:14.560
100% of the information

28:14.560 --> 28:15.120
perfectly.

28:15.680 --> 28:16.860
No history is lost.

28:17.700 --> 28:18.700
But the search cost

28:18.700 --> 28:20.220
to find a specific paragraph

28:20.220 --> 28:21.560
on a specific topic

28:21.560 --> 28:23.620
is so astronomically high

28:23.620 --> 28:24.460
that the books

28:24.460 --> 28:25.860
are effectively unreachable.

28:26.240 --> 28:26.980
The information

28:26.980 --> 28:28.080
might as well not exist.

28:28.400 --> 28:29.460
So by destroying

28:29.460 --> 28:30.060
the nuance

28:30.060 --> 28:31.080
of the book's contents

28:31.080 --> 28:32.540
and slapping a single

28:32.540 --> 28:33.720
highly compressed

28:33.720 --> 28:34.480
summary title

28:34.480 --> 28:35.140
on the spine,

28:35.260 --> 28:36.040
a collapse note,

28:36.200 --> 28:37.220
you impose structure

28:37.220 --> 28:37.920
on chaos.

28:38.100 --> 28:38.340
Yes.

28:38.920 --> 28:39.540
Language itself

28:39.540 --> 28:40.320
operates this way.

28:40.900 --> 28:41.660
The spoken word

28:41.660 --> 28:43.240
tree is a collapse note.

28:43.520 --> 28:43.980
How so?

28:44.340 --> 28:45.000
When I say tree,

28:45.260 --> 28:46.080
I am compressing

28:46.080 --> 28:47.040
millions of unique

28:47.040 --> 28:48.840
complex perceptual trajectories

28:48.840 --> 28:49.920
of individual leaves,

28:50.300 --> 28:50.920
rough bark,

28:51.360 --> 28:52.120
branching patterns,

28:52.320 --> 28:53.100
and shifting light

28:53.100 --> 28:54.340
into one single

28:54.340 --> 28:56.160
retrievable auditory token.

28:56.480 --> 28:57.580
Without collapse notes

28:57.580 --> 28:58.900
constantly destroying nuance

28:58.900 --> 29:00.380
to create manageable symbols,

29:00.800 --> 29:01.540
we wouldn't suffer

29:01.540 --> 29:02.580
from a lack of facts.

29:02.960 --> 29:03.420
We would suffer

29:03.420 --> 29:05.200
from total navigational failure.

29:05.340 --> 29:06.180
We would be paralyzed

29:06.180 --> 29:07.580
by the infinite fidelity

29:07.580 --> 29:08.280
of the universe.

29:08.440 --> 29:09.280
Which leads perfectly

29:09.280 --> 29:10.500
into the seventh aspect

29:10.500 --> 29:11.540
of this taxonomy.

29:11.860 --> 29:12.660
It's presented more

29:12.660 --> 29:13.260
as a theorem

29:13.260 --> 29:14.500
than a distinct class

29:14.500 --> 29:14.880
of notes,

29:15.100 --> 29:16.100
but it completely

29:16.100 --> 29:17.320
shattered my perspective.

29:17.740 --> 29:18.940
The ephemerality inversion.

29:19.560 --> 29:21.160
Theorem 13.5.

29:21.160 --> 29:23.320
It is a profound realization

29:23.320 --> 29:25.380
about how we evaluate history.

29:26.060 --> 29:26.520
Traditionally,

29:26.900 --> 29:27.700
in archaeology,

29:27.920 --> 29:28.440
in literature,

29:28.740 --> 29:29.480
in history,

29:29.480 --> 29:31.860
we inherently value notes

29:31.860 --> 29:32.540
that survive

29:32.540 --> 29:33.500
the test of time.

29:34.000 --> 29:35.040
Ancient Roman manuscripts,

29:35.820 --> 29:37.120
undelivered medieval letters,

29:37.740 --> 29:38.720
the Dead Sea Scrolls.

29:38.760 --> 29:40.480
We view their physical survival

29:40.480 --> 29:41.480
across millennia

29:41.480 --> 29:42.840
as the ultimate triumph

29:42.840 --> 29:43.900
of information.

29:44.380 --> 29:45.600
But the sphere pop theory

29:45.600 --> 29:46.460
of continuations

29:46.460 --> 29:47.920
argues the exact opposite.

29:48.420 --> 29:49.420
It argues that

29:49.420 --> 29:50.440
if a note's continuation

29:50.440 --> 29:51.620
is fully completed,

29:52.060 --> 29:53.260
its physical destruction

29:53.260 --> 29:54.560
actually represents

29:54.560 --> 29:55.640
optimal success.

29:55.820 --> 29:57.480
Think back to the grocery list

29:57.480 --> 29:58.000
we discussed

29:58.000 --> 29:58.740
at the very beginning.

29:59.220 --> 29:59.940
You write the list

29:59.940 --> 30:00.600
on your fridge.

30:00.920 --> 30:01.840
You drive to the store.

30:02.320 --> 30:03.380
You walk down the aisles.

30:03.520 --> 30:04.280
You put the milk,

30:04.480 --> 30:05.240
eggs, and bread

30:05.240 --> 30:05.840
in your cart.

30:06.260 --> 30:06.780
You pay.

30:07.220 --> 30:08.200
And as you walk out

30:08.200 --> 30:09.180
the automatic doors

30:09.180 --> 30:10.040
of the supermarket,

30:10.360 --> 30:11.860
you take that piece of paper

30:11.860 --> 30:12.580
and you throw it

30:12.580 --> 30:13.320
in the trash can.

30:13.540 --> 30:14.680
The destruction of that list

30:14.680 --> 30:15.200
does not mean

30:15.200 --> 30:15.960
the note failed.

30:16.480 --> 30:17.800
It means the note succeeded

30:17.800 --> 30:19.140
with absolute perfection.

30:19.400 --> 30:20.660
Its continuation volume,

30:20.920 --> 30:22.220
the future it was pointing toward,

30:22.460 --> 30:23.180
dropped to zero

30:23.180 --> 30:23.960
because that future

30:23.960 --> 30:24.580
was realized.

30:25.000 --> 30:26.260
It exhausted its purpose.

30:26.260 --> 30:27.660
So the ephemerality inversion

30:27.660 --> 30:29.020
states that the ancient notes

30:29.020 --> 30:30.300
we celebrate in museums,

30:30.680 --> 30:31.980
the detailed blueprints

30:31.980 --> 30:32.720
for castles

30:32.720 --> 30:33.800
that were never built,

30:34.400 --> 30:36.180
the agonizing drafts

30:36.180 --> 30:36.880
of novels

30:36.880 --> 30:37.980
that were never published,

30:38.340 --> 30:39.320
the desperate letters

30:39.320 --> 30:40.060
that never reached

30:40.060 --> 30:40.660
their recipient.

30:40.900 --> 30:42.440
They only survived physically

30:42.440 --> 30:44.060
because their intended futures

30:44.060 --> 30:45.120
were never realized.

30:45.460 --> 30:45.840
Wow.

30:46.020 --> 30:47.260
They are, in fact,

30:47.420 --> 30:49.220
monuments to trajectory failure.

30:49.540 --> 30:50.640
They are physical evidence

30:50.640 --> 30:52.080
of unrealized potential.

30:52.500 --> 30:53.880
The truly successful notes

30:53.880 --> 30:54.480
of antiquity,

30:54.480 --> 30:56.160
the ones that perfectly facilitated

30:56.160 --> 30:57.540
their intended continuations,

30:58.100 --> 30:59.840
left absolutely no trace

30:59.840 --> 31:01.220
in the archaeological record.

31:01.320 --> 31:01.560
Right.

31:01.580 --> 31:02.620
Because they were executed,

31:03.040 --> 31:04.360
their futures were realized,

31:04.500 --> 31:05.400
and they were discarded

31:05.400 --> 31:06.920
as the creators moved forward.

31:07.080 --> 31:08.580
That reframes every museum

31:08.580 --> 31:10.100
I have ever walked into.

31:10.360 --> 31:11.320
The exhibits are just

31:11.320 --> 31:11.900
a graveyard

31:11.900 --> 31:13.060
of interrupted futures.

31:13.060 --> 31:14.640
It is a sobering lens

31:14.640 --> 31:15.580
through which to view

31:15.580 --> 31:16.500
human artifacts.

31:17.280 --> 31:18.060
Let's pivot back

31:18.060 --> 31:19.580
to the machine side

31:19.580 --> 31:20.320
of this architecture,

31:20.700 --> 31:21.840
moving into section 5

31:21.840 --> 31:22.460
of the outline,

31:22.980 --> 31:23.860
operational semantics

31:23.860 --> 31:25.060
and the observation limit.

31:25.160 --> 31:25.740
Okay, let's get back

31:25.740 --> 31:26.200
to the computer.

31:26.500 --> 31:27.740
Because we have this beautiful,

31:27.960 --> 31:28.920
pristine theory

31:28.920 --> 31:30.280
of an ever-growing history

31:30.280 --> 31:30.920
of operators.

31:31.440 --> 31:32.580
But as a programmer,

31:33.160 --> 31:35.220
I have to push back hard here.

31:35.300 --> 31:36.360
I was waiting for this.

31:36.580 --> 31:37.760
If SpherePop claims

31:37.760 --> 31:39.440
to fundamentally abolish state,

31:39.440 --> 31:40.820
but ultimately

31:40.820 --> 31:41.760
it has to output

31:41.760 --> 31:42.760
a collapsed value

31:42.760 --> 31:43.520
for me to look at

31:43.520 --> 31:44.000
on a monitor,

31:44.320 --> 31:45.560
isn't that deeply hypocritical?

31:45.680 --> 31:46.180
Go on.

31:46.480 --> 31:47.680
If I write a SpherePop program

31:47.680 --> 31:48.840
to add 2 and 2

31:48.840 --> 31:50.100
and the screen prints

31:50.100 --> 31:50.780
the number 4,

31:50.960 --> 31:52.100
isn't 4 just a state?

31:52.580 --> 31:53.240
Aren't we just hiding

31:53.240 --> 31:54.940
the exact same state illusion

31:54.940 --> 31:56.040
behind a curtain

31:56.040 --> 31:57.260
of complex math?

31:57.540 --> 31:58.720
Plexian anticipated

31:58.720 --> 32:00.080
this exact pushback.

32:00.340 --> 32:00.940
In the text,

32:01.120 --> 32:02.560
it is explicitly referred to

32:02.560 --> 32:04.280
as the reviewer's challenge.

32:04.560 --> 32:05.420
So I'm not the only one.

32:05.620 --> 32:06.140
Not at all.

32:06.560 --> 32:07.480
It is the most obvious

32:07.480 --> 32:08.640
and necessary critique

32:08.640 --> 32:09.420
of the entire page.

32:09.440 --> 32:11.760
If the output looks like state,

32:11.880 --> 32:12.580
acts like state,

32:12.680 --> 32:14.120
and is consumed like state,

32:14.220 --> 32:15.100
isn't it just state?

32:15.240 --> 32:15.480
Right.

32:15.580 --> 32:16.980
You can't claim to abolish something

32:16.980 --> 32:18.040
and then hand it to me

32:18.040 --> 32:19.420
as the final product.

32:19.540 --> 32:20.400
But the author answers

32:20.400 --> 32:21.060
this challenge

32:21.060 --> 32:22.820
with theorem 17.2,

32:23.240 --> 32:24.860
the no direct observation theorem.

32:25.740 --> 32:26.560
This theorem proves

32:26.560 --> 32:27.400
that any system

32:27.400 --> 32:28.640
that must output information

32:28.640 --> 32:30.560
into a finite observable space

32:30.560 --> 32:32.600
must generate a state illusion.

32:33.140 --> 32:34.500
It's not a bad engineering choice.

32:34.920 --> 32:35.280
No.

32:35.420 --> 32:36.660
It is an inescapable

32:36.660 --> 32:38.460
mathematical cardinality constraint.

32:38.460 --> 32:40.320
Break down the cardinality constraint for me.

32:40.400 --> 32:41.060
What does that mean

32:41.060 --> 32:41.820
in plain English?

32:42.140 --> 32:42.740
It comes down

32:42.740 --> 32:43.920
to the pigeonhole principle

32:43.920 --> 32:44.660
in mathematics.

32:44.980 --> 32:46.020
If you have 10 pigeons

32:46.020 --> 32:47.000
and 9 holes,

32:47.160 --> 32:48.120
at least 2 pigeons

32:48.120 --> 32:48.980
must share a hole.

32:49.240 --> 32:49.540
Okay.

32:49.820 --> 32:50.240
Simple enough.

32:50.580 --> 32:52.500
The space of all possible histories

32:52.500 --> 32:53.820
in a sphere pot program

32:53.820 --> 32:55.400
is infinitely large.

32:56.040 --> 32:57.560
Every microscopic operation

32:57.560 --> 32:58.520
branches the tree

32:58.520 --> 32:59.440
of history further.

32:59.880 --> 33:00.700
But the space

33:00.700 --> 33:01.720
on your computer screen,

33:01.840 --> 33:02.600
the grid of pixels,

33:02.860 --> 33:04.080
the finite memory buffer

33:04.080 --> 33:05.660
allocated to display a result

33:05.660 --> 33:07.020
is strictly limited.

33:07.260 --> 33:08.460
So you simply cannot map

33:08.460 --> 33:09.380
an infinite number

33:09.380 --> 33:10.340
of unique histories

33:10.340 --> 33:11.980
onto a finite number

33:11.980 --> 33:12.860
of display pixels

33:12.860 --> 33:13.940
without mapping

33:13.940 --> 33:15.140
multiple different histories

33:15.140 --> 33:17.520
to the exact same visual output.

33:17.640 --> 33:17.960
Exactly.

33:18.100 --> 33:19.040
So if history A

33:19.040 --> 33:20.440
adds 2 plus 2

33:20.440 --> 33:21.160
to get 4

33:21.160 --> 33:22.440
and history B

33:22.440 --> 33:23.700
subtracts 1 from 5

33:23.700 --> 33:24.380
to get 4,

33:24.560 --> 33:25.640
your finite screen

33:25.640 --> 33:26.960
just shows the pixel pattern

33:26.960 --> 33:27.480
for 4.

33:27.920 --> 33:29.840
It physically cannot display

33:29.840 --> 33:30.460
the infinite,

33:30.860 --> 33:31.840
invisible nuance

33:31.840 --> 33:32.820
of the trajectory

33:32.820 --> 33:33.760
that arrived at 4.

33:33.760 --> 33:34.880
The output medium

33:34.880 --> 33:35.920
forces a collapse.

33:36.280 --> 33:36.540
Therefore,

33:36.860 --> 33:38.000
the crucial distinction

33:38.000 --> 33:38.980
Flyxion introduces

33:38.980 --> 33:39.720
is the difference

33:39.720 --> 33:40.620
between state erasure

33:40.620 --> 33:41.860
and state illusion.

33:42.360 --> 33:42.620
Yes.

33:42.920 --> 33:43.700
And this is key.

33:44.200 --> 33:45.860
Traditional computer architectures

33:45.860 --> 33:46.940
like the von Neumann

33:46.940 --> 33:48.160
architecture we use today

33:48.160 --> 33:49.900
suffer from state erasure.

33:50.280 --> 33:51.200
They compute the 4,

33:51.400 --> 33:52.380
they write it to memory,

33:52.600 --> 33:53.660
and they permanently

33:53.660 --> 33:54.820
physically delete

33:54.820 --> 33:56.500
the history of the 2 plus 2

33:56.500 --> 33:57.700
or the 5 minus 1.

33:57.840 --> 33:58.760
But sphere pop

33:58.760 --> 34:00.840
eliminates state erasure.

34:01.220 --> 34:02.280
The underlying history

34:02.280 --> 34:03.680
is always perfectly preserved

34:03.680 --> 34:05.180
and mathematically accessible

34:05.180 --> 34:06.300
within the deep system.

34:06.780 --> 34:07.860
But sphere pop cannot

34:07.860 --> 34:09.700
eliminate the state illusion

34:09.700 --> 34:11.280
because for a human being

34:11.280 --> 34:12.640
to actually look at the result,

34:13.000 --> 34:14.020
we still have to look through

34:14.020 --> 34:15.700
a finite, degenerate lens.

34:16.300 --> 34:17.540
We are forced to use

34:17.540 --> 34:18.600
the collapse operator.

34:18.760 --> 34:19.880
So an observable state

34:19.880 --> 34:20.980
isn't a physical thing

34:20.980 --> 34:21.880
sitting on a hard drive.

34:22.260 --> 34:23.980
State is just an equivalence class.

34:24.220 --> 34:25.260
It's a temporary filter

34:25.260 --> 34:26.560
applied over the history.

34:26.880 --> 34:27.960
And the source details

34:27.960 --> 34:29.580
four canonical collapse rules,

34:29.780 --> 34:30.640
four different lenses

34:30.640 --> 34:31.380
that you can choose

34:31.380 --> 34:31.920
to look through.

34:31.920 --> 34:32.820
The system allows

34:32.820 --> 34:33.320
a programmer

34:33.320 --> 34:34.560
to explicitly choose

34:34.560 --> 34:35.240
how the history

34:35.240 --> 34:35.900
has collapsed

34:35.900 --> 34:36.780
for observation.

34:37.580 --> 34:38.640
The four primary lenses

34:38.640 --> 34:39.480
are identity,

34:39.980 --> 34:40.500
last write,

34:40.900 --> 34:41.300
accumulate,

34:41.740 --> 34:42.340
and projection.

34:42.740 --> 34:43.620
Let's ground these.

34:44.160 --> 34:44.820
Imagine I have

34:44.820 --> 34:46.020
a bank account ledger.

34:46.260 --> 34:47.060
I deposit $10,

34:47.520 --> 34:48.400
I withdraw $5,

34:48.800 --> 34:49.620
I deposit $20.

34:50.440 --> 34:52.020
How do these four lenses

34:52.020 --> 34:53.280
change what I see?

34:53.600 --> 34:54.140
Let's start

34:54.140 --> 34:55.100
with the identity rule.

34:55.740 --> 34:56.740
It is the finest,

34:56.960 --> 34:58.160
most transparent lens.

34:58.640 --> 34:59.420
It simply says,

34:59.740 --> 35:00.780
show me everything.

35:00.780 --> 35:01.800
If you apply

35:01.800 --> 35:02.820
the identity collapse

35:02.820 --> 35:03.740
to your bank account,

35:04.180 --> 35:05.040
the output is

35:05.040 --> 35:05.540
the raw,

35:05.700 --> 35:06.800
unadulterated history.

35:07.300 --> 35:08.400
The exact sequence

35:08.400 --> 35:09.720
of the $10 deposit,

35:10.000 --> 35:11.180
the $5 withdrawal,

35:11.400 --> 35:12.740
and the $20 deposit,

35:13.100 --> 35:14.840
with all their timestamps

35:14.840 --> 35:16.560
and cryptographic proofs attached.

35:16.800 --> 35:17.960
Which is great for an audit,

35:18.200 --> 35:19.940
but probably way too overwhelming

35:19.940 --> 35:20.680
for just checking

35:20.680 --> 35:21.780
my balance at an ATM.

35:22.020 --> 35:22.920
Exactly the problem.

35:23.300 --> 35:24.080
The second lens

35:24.080 --> 35:25.120
is the last write rule.

35:25.560 --> 35:27.060
This mimics the traditional state

35:27.060 --> 35:27.760
we are used to

35:27.760 --> 35:28.900
in everyday programming.

35:28.900 --> 35:30.660
It forcefully collapses

35:30.660 --> 35:31.420
the history down

35:31.420 --> 35:32.440
to only the most recent

35:32.440 --> 35:33.000
pop event

35:33.000 --> 35:34.000
for a given symbol.

35:34.400 --> 35:35.740
So if a variable changed

35:35.740 --> 35:37.160
from 1 to 2 to 3,

35:37.580 --> 35:38.740
the last write lens

35:38.740 --> 35:39.920
only shows you the 3.

35:40.280 --> 35:41.740
It hides the journey entirely.

35:42.000 --> 35:42.920
But the crucial difference

35:42.920 --> 35:43.640
is that the journey

35:43.640 --> 35:45.060
is still safely hidden underneath,

35:45.400 --> 35:45.620
right?

35:45.900 --> 35:47.180
The system hasn't deleted

35:47.180 --> 35:48.040
the 1 and the 2,

35:48.120 --> 35:48.720
you've just chosen

35:48.720 --> 35:49.500
to filter them

35:49.500 --> 35:50.420
out of your current view.

35:50.660 --> 35:52.080
That is the defining distinction.

35:52.580 --> 35:53.560
The third lens

35:53.560 --> 35:54.740
is the accumulate rule.

35:55.220 --> 35:56.280
This maps a symbol

35:56.280 --> 35:57.880
to a mathematical reduction

35:57.880 --> 35:58.860
of its events.

35:59.100 --> 36:00.340
In your bank account example,

36:00.560 --> 36:01.560
the accumulate lens

36:01.560 --> 36:02.720
would take the plus 10,

36:02.880 --> 36:03.580
the minus 5,

36:03.600 --> 36:04.360
and the plus 20,

36:04.760 --> 36:06.040
run a summation function,

36:06.500 --> 36:07.860
and output the final balance

36:07.860 --> 36:08.440
of $25.

36:08.880 --> 36:09.760
And the fourth lens,

36:10.180 --> 36:11.080
the projection rule.

36:11.280 --> 36:12.200
The projection lens

36:12.200 --> 36:13.200
filters the history

36:13.200 --> 36:14.700
based on specific metadata

36:14.700 --> 36:15.600
or tags.

36:16.060 --> 36:17.120
It might only show events

36:17.120 --> 36:17.580
that match

36:17.580 --> 36:19.200
a specific security clearance

36:19.200 --> 36:20.640
or events generated

36:20.640 --> 36:21.800
by a specific user.

36:22.000 --> 36:23.040
The grand takeaway here

36:23.040 --> 36:23.700
is that state

36:23.700 --> 36:25.360
is never a fundamental reality.

36:26.200 --> 36:27.220
State depends entirely

36:27.220 --> 36:27.900
on which of these

36:27.900 --> 36:28.820
mathematical lenses

36:28.820 --> 36:30.220
you choose to bolt

36:30.220 --> 36:30.880
onto the front

36:30.880 --> 36:31.500
of your history.

36:31.740 --> 36:32.960
Beautifully summarized.

36:33.100 --> 36:34.140
Okay, this brings up

36:34.140 --> 36:35.440
a massive structural question

36:35.440 --> 36:36.620
moving into section 6

36:36.620 --> 36:37.280
on type theory.

36:37.860 --> 36:39.340
If state is just a lens

36:39.340 --> 36:40.440
and underneath it

36:40.440 --> 36:41.800
is this massive chaotic

36:41.800 --> 36:42.700
ocean of history,

36:43.320 --> 36:44.200
how do we ensure

36:44.200 --> 36:45.040
that these collapses

36:45.040 --> 36:46.040
don't just output

36:46.040 --> 36:46.920
total garbage?

36:47.260 --> 36:48.160
To maintain order,

36:48.280 --> 36:49.320
the system needs rules.

36:50.140 --> 36:51.580
But types in SpherePop,

36:51.580 --> 36:52.360
the way the system

36:52.360 --> 36:53.340
categorizes data

36:53.340 --> 36:54.920
are fundamentally different

36:54.920 --> 36:56.160
from types in normal languages

36:56.160 --> 36:57.560
like Python or Java.

36:58.040 --> 36:58.920
In traditional languages,

36:59.120 --> 37:00.620
a type like an integer,

37:01.120 --> 37:02.460
a boolean, or a string

37:02.460 --> 37:03.700
is just a definition

37:03.700 --> 37:05.640
of a set of admissible values.

37:06.280 --> 37:07.860
If you declare a variable

37:07.860 --> 37:08.540
as an integer,

37:09.140 --> 37:10.220
the compiler ensures

37:10.220 --> 37:11.500
it holds a 1

37:11.500 --> 37:12.960
or a 5 or 100

37:12.960 --> 37:14.700
but not the word apple.

37:14.820 --> 37:16.400
That is a value set type system.

37:16.500 --> 37:17.640
But in a history primary

37:17.640 --> 37:19.240
architecture like SpherePop,

37:19.240 --> 37:21.100
merely verifying

37:21.100 --> 37:22.140
the final value

37:22.140 --> 37:23.600
is completely insufficient.

37:24.300 --> 37:25.060
Why is it insufficient?

37:25.220 --> 37:26.060
I mean, if it's a number,

37:26.240 --> 37:26.700
it's a number.

37:26.820 --> 37:28.600
Because a value in SpherePop

37:28.600 --> 37:30.360
does not exist in a vacuum.

37:30.640 --> 37:31.940
It is the terminal point

37:31.940 --> 37:33.540
of a specific path

37:33.540 --> 37:34.300
through history.

37:34.900 --> 37:36.260
So a type in SpherePop

37:36.260 --> 37:37.460
is not just a label,

37:37.780 --> 37:39.820
it is an admissibility certificate.

37:40.060 --> 37:40.980
An admissibility certificate?

37:41.000 --> 37:42.200
A value of type T

37:42.200 --> 37:45.260
is a formal cryptographic proof

37:45.260 --> 37:46.720
that the historical path

37:46.720 --> 37:47.580
taken to arrive

37:47.580 --> 37:48.840
at this specific value

37:48.840 --> 37:50.040
did not violate

37:50.040 --> 37:51.280
any system constraints

37:51.280 --> 37:51.940
along the way.

37:52.240 --> 37:53.300
The type certifies

37:53.300 --> 37:54.740
the legitimacy of the journey,

37:54.960 --> 37:55.880
not just the shape

37:55.880 --> 37:56.700
of the destination.

37:56.940 --> 37:57.440
Let me see if I can

37:57.440 --> 37:58.000
translate that.

37:58.280 --> 37:58.900
If I have a variable

37:58.900 --> 37:59.860
in a traditional program

37:59.860 --> 38:01.320
that represents a password file,

38:01.440 --> 38:02.140
the type just says

38:02.140 --> 38:03.260
this is a string of text.

38:03.600 --> 38:04.740
But in SpherePop,

38:05.220 --> 38:06.340
the type doesn't just say

38:06.340 --> 38:07.220
this is text.

38:07.380 --> 38:09.220
The type is a historical passport.

38:09.780 --> 38:11.220
It says this is text

38:11.220 --> 38:13.020
and here is the mathematical proof

38:13.020 --> 38:14.900
that you had administrator permissions

38:14.900 --> 38:15.900
when you asked for it.

38:15.900 --> 38:16.800
Here's the proof

38:16.800 --> 38:17.960
the file actually existed

38:17.960 --> 38:18.640
on the drive

38:18.640 --> 38:19.780
and here's the proof

38:19.780 --> 38:20.760
that the read operation

38:20.760 --> 38:22.180
completed without corruption.

38:22.920 --> 38:24.440
That is a brilliant translation.

38:25.200 --> 38:26.200
The type is a passport

38:26.200 --> 38:27.920
covered in verified stamps.

38:28.420 --> 38:29.980
And this heavy reliance

38:29.980 --> 38:31.020
on historical proof

38:31.020 --> 38:32.240
leads to one of the most

38:32.240 --> 38:33.420
fascinating tangents

38:33.420 --> 38:34.340
in the monograph.

38:34.540 --> 38:35.400
Oh, about the interpreter.

38:35.680 --> 38:36.120
Yes.

38:36.580 --> 38:37.360
The author details

38:37.360 --> 38:38.480
a specific conflict

38:38.480 --> 38:39.140
they encountered

38:39.140 --> 38:39.940
while building

38:39.940 --> 38:41.060
the SpherePop interpreter,

38:41.620 --> 38:42.400
a conflict between

38:42.400 --> 38:43.360
the interpreter engine

38:43.360 --> 38:44.300
and the type checker

38:44.300 --> 38:45.800
regarding free variables.

38:46.140 --> 38:47.100
Yeah, I flagged that section.

38:47.200 --> 38:47.700
It sounded like

38:47.700 --> 38:48.980
they ran into a nasty bug

38:48.980 --> 38:49.780
and ended up turning it

38:49.780 --> 38:50.580
into a profound

38:50.580 --> 38:51.620
philosophical feature.

38:52.080 --> 38:53.380
What exactly was the conflict?

38:53.680 --> 38:54.320
It comes down

38:54.320 --> 38:56.480
to a deep epistemological choice

38:56.480 --> 38:57.340
regarding open

38:57.340 --> 38:58.540
versus closed worlds.

38:58.940 --> 38:59.940
When the type checker

38:59.940 --> 39:00.940
is scanning the code

39:00.940 --> 39:01.860
and it encounters

39:01.860 --> 39:03.060
a variable or a symbol

39:03.060 --> 39:03.820
that has never been

39:03.820 --> 39:04.600
declared before,

39:04.720 --> 39:05.780
a free variable,

39:06.000 --> 39:06.700
what should it do?

39:07.000 --> 39:08.180
Well, in my experience,

39:08.460 --> 39:09.660
the compiler immediately

39:09.660 --> 39:10.620
throws a red error

39:10.620 --> 39:11.340
and stops,

39:12.000 --> 39:13.080
variable undefined.

39:13.420 --> 39:14.740
That reaction is based

39:14.740 --> 39:16.220
on a closed world assumption.

39:16.960 --> 39:18.320
A closed world assumes

39:18.320 --> 39:19.060
that the compiler

39:19.060 --> 39:20.160
possesses complete,

39:20.420 --> 39:21.120
total knowledge

39:21.120 --> 39:21.700
of the universe.

39:22.540 --> 39:23.540
If the system

39:23.540 --> 39:24.640
doesn't know about something,

39:24.960 --> 39:26.180
it concludes that the thing

39:26.180 --> 39:27.980
fundamentally does not exist.

39:28.680 --> 39:30.460
Therefore, referencing it

39:30.460 --> 39:31.300
is an illegal,

39:31.560 --> 39:32.720
inadmissible act.

39:32.980 --> 39:34.260
But an open world context

39:34.260 --> 39:34.900
is different.

39:35.120 --> 39:36.440
An open world context

39:36.440 --> 39:38.080
assumes partial knowledge.

39:38.480 --> 39:39.620
It acknowledges that

39:39.620 --> 39:40.620
the system is embedded

39:40.620 --> 39:41.420
in a larger,

39:41.620 --> 39:42.880
unpredictable universe.

39:43.500 --> 39:44.940
Under an open world assumption,

39:45.420 --> 39:46.680
the absence of a declaration

39:46.680 --> 39:47.260
doesn't mean

39:47.260 --> 39:48.160
this doesn't exist.

39:48.540 --> 39:49.180
It just means

39:49.180 --> 39:50.920
this is an undecided possibility.

39:51.240 --> 39:51.900
Let me try to build

39:51.900 --> 39:52.880
an analogy for this.

39:53.140 --> 39:53.740
It's the difference

39:53.740 --> 39:54.500
between ordering

39:54.500 --> 39:55.420
in a strict restaurant

39:55.420 --> 39:56.180
versus hosting

39:56.180 --> 39:57.200
a neighborhood potluck.

39:57.400 --> 39:58.280
Oh, I like this.

39:58.420 --> 39:59.420
If I am at a restaurant

39:59.420 --> 40:00.500
with a closed world menu

40:00.500 --> 40:01.760
and I try to order a taco,

40:01.980 --> 40:02.620
but tacos aren't

40:02.620 --> 40:03.500
on the printed menu,

40:04.120 --> 40:04.820
the waiter says,

40:05.000 --> 40:06.560
error, item, undefined.

40:06.660 --> 40:07.800
You cannot order that.

40:07.800 --> 40:08.800
The menu is

40:08.800 --> 40:09.820
the entire universe.

40:10.320 --> 40:11.360
But if I am hosting

40:11.360 --> 40:12.700
an open world potluck

40:12.700 --> 40:14.400
and someone walks in

40:14.400 --> 40:15.120
carrying a dish

40:15.120 --> 40:16.140
I've never seen before,

40:16.380 --> 40:17.420
I don't throw an error

40:17.420 --> 40:18.220
and kick them out.

40:18.720 --> 40:20.000
The absence of the dish

40:20.000 --> 40:21.040
from my initial plan

40:21.040 --> 40:22.360
just means my knowledge

40:22.360 --> 40:23.120
was incomplete.

40:23.980 --> 40:25.200
I accept the new dish

40:25.200 --> 40:27.060
as an undecided possibility

40:27.060 --> 40:27.980
entering the system.

40:28.260 --> 40:28.960
That captures

40:28.960 --> 40:30.220
the epistemological divide

40:30.220 --> 40:30.760
perfectly.

40:31.300 --> 40:31.880
Sphere Pop

40:31.880 --> 40:33.020
forces the programmer

40:33.020 --> 40:34.560
to explicitly declare

40:34.560 --> 40:35.760
which world boundary

40:35.760 --> 40:36.480
is in force.

40:36.480 --> 40:37.500
You have to tell

40:37.500 --> 40:38.020
the compiler

40:38.020 --> 40:39.020
whether it is operating

40:39.020 --> 40:39.860
in a closed,

40:40.040 --> 40:41.220
strictly controlled restaurant

40:41.220 --> 40:42.480
or an open,

40:42.640 --> 40:43.720
dynamic potluck.

40:43.960 --> 40:44.680
And this fundamentally

40:44.680 --> 40:46.000
ties into how the system

40:46.000 --> 40:47.420
handles errors natively

40:47.420 --> 40:48.520
through a mechanism

40:48.520 --> 40:50.020
called proof-carrying refusal.

40:50.240 --> 40:51.220
We talked about refusal

40:51.220 --> 40:52.780
earlier as documenting

40:52.780 --> 40:54.260
a tiger behind a door.

40:54.820 --> 40:55.640
How does proof-carrying

40:55.640 --> 40:56.440
refusal work

40:56.440 --> 40:58.340
when code actually breaks?

40:58.620 --> 40:59.740
In a standard program

40:59.740 --> 41:01.240
when a fatal error occurs,

41:01.400 --> 41:02.940
say, trying to divide by zero,

41:03.320 --> 41:04.560
the program either crashes

41:04.560 --> 41:06.480
silently or it throws an exception

41:06.480 --> 41:07.720
that unwinds and destroys

41:07.720 --> 41:08.540
the stacked race.

41:08.960 --> 41:09.560
In Sphere Pop,

41:09.860 --> 41:10.740
the inadmissibility

41:10.740 --> 41:11.560
is carried forward

41:11.560 --> 41:12.260
into the future

41:12.260 --> 41:13.420
as a formal proof.

41:13.940 --> 41:15.000
When the error occurs,

41:15.120 --> 41:15.640
it is recorded

41:15.640 --> 41:16.740
as a refuse event.

41:17.200 --> 41:17.940
But this event

41:17.940 --> 41:18.780
isn't a crash.

41:19.040 --> 41:19.760
The error itself

41:19.760 --> 41:21.320
becomes a first-class piece

41:21.320 --> 41:22.740
of type-level information.

41:23.280 --> 41:24.540
It carries the cryptographic

41:24.540 --> 41:26.160
evidence of its own failure.

41:26.640 --> 41:27.900
So the system doesn't stop.

41:28.440 --> 41:29.180
It just holds up

41:29.180 --> 41:30.240
a mathematical certificate

41:30.240 --> 41:30.820
that says,

41:31.260 --> 41:32.160
I tried to do this

41:32.160 --> 41:33.240
and here's exactly

41:33.240 --> 41:33.900
why it broke,

41:34.300 --> 41:35.320
but what if I want

41:35.320 --> 41:36.080
to write code

41:36.080 --> 41:37.080
to fix that error?

41:37.700 --> 41:38.600
Handling an error

41:38.600 --> 41:39.520
in this architecture

41:39.520 --> 41:40.640
requires you to generate

41:40.640 --> 41:41.760
a recovery certificate.

41:42.220 --> 41:43.000
It is essentially

41:43.000 --> 41:44.460
the computational equivalent

41:44.460 --> 41:45.640
of a repair note

41:45.640 --> 41:46.360
from the taxonomy

41:46.360 --> 41:47.000
we discussed.

41:47.260 --> 41:48.140
A recovery certificate

41:48.140 --> 41:49.480
is a mathematical proof

41:49.480 --> 41:50.820
that formally transforms

41:50.820 --> 41:52.860
the documented inadmissibility,

41:53.080 --> 41:53.900
the error state,

41:54.340 --> 41:55.120
into a valid

41:55.120 --> 41:56.400
admissible continuation.

41:57.000 --> 41:58.240
You aren't just using

41:58.240 --> 41:59.220
a try-catch block

41:59.220 --> 42:00.100
to sweep the crash

42:00.100 --> 42:00.660
under the rug.

42:00.820 --> 42:01.440
You are appending

42:01.440 --> 42:02.560
a certified patch

42:02.560 --> 42:04.080
to the permanent history log

42:04.080 --> 42:05.800
showing exactly what broke

42:05.800 --> 42:07.340
and mathematically proving

42:07.340 --> 42:08.240
how your new code

42:08.240 --> 42:09.840
safely bypasses the failure.

42:10.240 --> 42:11.840
This sounds incredibly robust,

42:12.280 --> 42:12.920
but it brings up

42:12.920 --> 42:13.980
an absolutely massive

42:13.980 --> 42:14.960
practical problem,

42:15.320 --> 42:16.020
which leads us

42:16.020 --> 42:16.920
to Section 7,

42:17.460 --> 42:18.240
Garbage Collection

42:18.240 --> 42:19.580
and Compiler Correctness.

42:19.680 --> 42:20.660
The elephant in the room.

42:20.820 --> 42:22.240
If histories never die

42:22.240 --> 42:23.940
and every single

42:23.940 --> 42:25.020
variable assignment,

42:25.200 --> 42:26.440
every branch taken,

42:26.800 --> 42:27.940
every condition checked,

42:27.940 --> 42:29.660
and every error encountered

42:29.660 --> 42:31.280
is kept as a permanent,

42:31.660 --> 42:33.340
cryptographically certified record,

42:33.900 --> 42:34.140
I mean,

42:34.200 --> 42:34.760
the listener's probably

42:34.760 --> 42:35.620
screaming right now,

42:35.860 --> 42:37.040
how does a computer

42:37.040 --> 42:37.740
running this

42:37.740 --> 42:39.040
not run out of RAM

42:39.040 --> 42:39.820
in five seconds?

42:39.820 --> 42:41.800
The memory explosion problem

42:41.800 --> 42:42.740
is the crucible

42:42.740 --> 42:44.740
for trajectory-based computation.

42:45.460 --> 42:46.780
Traditional garbage collection,

42:47.040 --> 42:47.680
or GC,

42:48.020 --> 42:49.200
is relatively simple.

42:49.340 --> 42:50.160
The runtime engine

42:50.160 --> 42:51.000
scans the memory,

42:51.440 --> 42:52.600
looks for data objects

42:52.600 --> 42:53.280
that the program

42:53.280 --> 42:54.420
is no longer pointing to,

42:54.720 --> 42:55.760
and simply deletes them

42:55.760 --> 42:56.640
to free up space.

42:56.640 --> 42:57.980
Because if nothing is looking at it,

42:58.020 --> 42:59.040
it doesn't exist anymore.

42:59.240 --> 42:59.960
But in SpherePop,

43:00.300 --> 43:01.920
GC is completely redefined

43:01.920 --> 43:04.080
as admissibility-guided collapse.

43:04.280 --> 43:04.480
Yeah.

43:04.700 --> 43:05.640
You cannot simply

43:05.640 --> 43:06.780
delete historical events

43:06.780 --> 43:08.620
just because no current variable

43:08.620 --> 43:09.860
is directly pointing to them.

43:09.900 --> 43:11.600
Because that deep history

43:11.600 --> 43:12.880
might contain

43:12.880 --> 43:14.300
the exact admissibility

43:14.300 --> 43:15.320
certificates required

43:15.320 --> 43:16.000
to prove that

43:16.000 --> 43:16.860
a future collapse

43:16.860 --> 43:17.880
operation is valid.

43:18.320 --> 43:19.180
If you delete

43:19.180 --> 43:19.600
the history

43:19.600 --> 43:20.700
of the passport stamps,

43:20.960 --> 43:22.160
you can no longer prove

43:22.160 --> 43:23.180
the passport is real

43:23.180 --> 43:24.020
when you hit the border.

43:24.020 --> 43:25.140
Because the type checker

43:25.140 --> 43:26.480
relies on the entire journey

43:26.480 --> 43:27.240
to prove safety,

43:27.380 --> 43:28.600
so how do you delete anything?

43:29.180 --> 43:30.460
GC in SpherePop

43:30.460 --> 43:31.760
is the delicate art

43:31.760 --> 43:32.460
of collapsing

43:32.460 --> 43:33.740
dead segments of history

43:33.740 --> 43:34.540
to save space,

43:34.740 --> 43:36.360
while rigidly preserving

43:36.360 --> 43:37.500
the structural certificates

43:37.500 --> 43:38.720
required to satisfy

43:38.720 --> 43:40.100
future type checks.

43:40.560 --> 43:41.860
The monograph outlines

43:41.860 --> 43:43.720
three specific GC strategies

43:43.720 --> 43:44.520
that trade off

43:44.520 --> 43:45.580
between memory efficiency

43:45.580 --> 43:47.200
and historical fidelity.

43:47.580 --> 43:48.380
The three are

43:48.380 --> 43:48.900
MarkScan,

43:49.040 --> 43:49.380
Quotient,

43:49.440 --> 43:50.400
and Certificate only.

43:50.800 --> 43:51.780
Break these down for us.

43:52.000 --> 43:53.560
MarkScan is the most conservative,

43:53.560 --> 43:54.720
and the most expensive.

43:55.140 --> 43:56.100
It traverses the entire

43:56.100 --> 43:56.760
history tree

43:56.760 --> 43:57.920
and preserves absolutely

43:57.920 --> 43:58.840
everything that is

43:58.840 --> 43:59.720
mathematically linked

43:59.720 --> 44:00.660
to a potential future.

44:00.940 --> 44:02.300
It maintains perfect fidelity,

44:02.640 --> 44:03.220
but it consumes

44:03.220 --> 44:04.340
vast amounts of memory.

44:04.540 --> 44:05.340
And Quotient GC.

44:05.820 --> 44:06.540
Quotient GC,

44:06.680 --> 44:07.220
on the other hand,

44:07.240 --> 44:07.840
is the closest

44:07.840 --> 44:08.780
to what we do today.

44:09.340 --> 44:10.640
It forcefully collapses

44:10.640 --> 44:11.600
sections of history

44:11.600 --> 44:12.340
down to just

44:12.340 --> 44:13.620
their last known state.

44:14.100 --> 44:15.720
It saves massive amounts

44:15.720 --> 44:16.160
of memory,

44:16.520 --> 44:17.280
but it permanently

44:17.280 --> 44:18.420
destroys your ability

44:18.420 --> 44:19.720
to rewind and replay

44:19.720 --> 44:21.380
that specific section

44:21.380 --> 44:22.100
of the trajectory.

44:22.100 --> 44:23.660
So Quotient GC

44:23.660 --> 44:24.920
is admitting defeat

44:24.920 --> 44:25.660
and returning

44:25.660 --> 44:26.580
to the state illusion

44:26.580 --> 44:27.560
to save RAM.

44:28.040 --> 44:29.000
What about the third one,

44:29.220 --> 44:29.960
Certificate Only?

44:30.520 --> 44:31.760
Certificate Only GC

44:31.760 --> 44:32.860
is the uniquely

44:32.860 --> 44:34.320
sphere-pop approach.

44:34.880 --> 44:35.980
It analyzes

44:35.980 --> 44:37.180
a historical trajectory

44:37.180 --> 44:38.380
and deletes

44:38.380 --> 44:39.940
the actual raw values

44:39.940 --> 44:40.920
and the pop events,

44:41.100 --> 44:42.420
the heavy data payloads,

44:42.460 --> 44:43.320
to save memory.

44:43.720 --> 44:45.060
But it rigidly extracts

44:45.060 --> 44:46.700
and keeps all the refuse proofs

44:46.700 --> 44:47.500
and the type certificates.

44:47.860 --> 44:48.560
It throws away

44:48.560 --> 44:49.480
the beta you don't need,

44:49.560 --> 44:49.920
but it keeps

44:49.920 --> 44:51.000
the mathematical skeleton

44:51.000 --> 44:52.100
that guarantees the data

44:52.100 --> 44:53.220
was processed legally.

44:53.500 --> 44:54.260
That is fascinating.

44:54.380 --> 44:55.060
It's like throwing away

44:55.060 --> 44:55.800
the heavy textbook

44:55.800 --> 44:56.960
but keeping the index

44:56.960 --> 44:58.020
in the table of contents

44:58.020 --> 44:59.180
so you still know

44:59.180 --> 44:59.680
the structure

44:59.680 --> 45:00.380
of what was there.

45:00.500 --> 45:01.660
And this intense focus

45:01.660 --> 45:02.400
on preserving

45:02.400 --> 45:03.600
the structure of history

45:03.600 --> 45:05.040
completely changes

45:05.040 --> 45:05.700
the definition

45:05.700 --> 45:06.860
of a correct compiler.

45:07.340 --> 45:08.000
The source has

45:08.000 --> 45:08.680
a whole section

45:08.680 --> 45:10.440
contrasting compiler correctness

45:10.440 --> 45:11.500
in traditional languages

45:11.500 --> 45:12.920
versus sphere-pop.

45:13.200 --> 45:13.560
Yes.

45:14.080 --> 45:15.420
In traditional compilation,

45:15.900 --> 45:16.680
the goal standard

45:16.680 --> 45:18.280
is output equivalence.

45:18.280 --> 45:20.640
If you write a program in C

45:20.640 --> 45:21.480
that calculates

45:21.480 --> 45:22.540
a complex algorithm

45:22.540 --> 45:24.020
and outputs the number 42

45:24.020 --> 45:25.240
and you run it

45:25.240 --> 45:25.920
through a compiler,

45:26.380 --> 45:27.400
the compiler's job

45:27.400 --> 45:28.760
is to generate machine code.

45:29.280 --> 45:30.620
As long as that machine code

45:30.620 --> 45:32.280
also outputs the number 42

45:32.280 --> 45:33.540
under the same conditions,

45:34.080 --> 45:35.260
the compiler is considered

45:35.260 --> 45:36.440
mathematically correct.

45:36.860 --> 45:37.820
It doesn't matter

45:37.820 --> 45:38.480
if the compiler

45:38.480 --> 45:39.600
rearranged the loops,

45:39.920 --> 45:41.200
deleted unused variables,

45:41.480 --> 45:42.500
or took a completely

45:42.500 --> 45:43.220
different path

45:43.220 --> 45:43.800
under the hood

45:43.800 --> 45:44.860
to optimize speed.

45:45.120 --> 45:46.240
As long as the destination

45:46.240 --> 45:47.040
is the same,

45:47.320 --> 45:48.140
it is correct.

45:48.280 --> 45:49.460
But in sphere-pop,

45:49.840 --> 45:50.360
the compiler

45:50.360 --> 45:51.100
doesn't output

45:51.100 --> 45:52.140
raw machine code.

45:52.460 --> 45:53.300
It outputs an event

45:53.300 --> 45:54.600
intermediate representation,

45:55.060 --> 45:55.700
an event IR,

45:56.440 --> 45:57.200
and the author states

45:57.200 --> 45:58.040
it must achieve

45:58.040 --> 45:59.180
history equivalence.

45:59.300 --> 46:00.580
This is a radical shift.

46:00.820 --> 46:01.340
In sphere-pop,

46:01.500 --> 46:02.100
two programs

46:02.100 --> 46:02.520
that arrive

46:02.520 --> 46:03.900
at the exact same answer

46:03.900 --> 46:04.620
are fundamentally

46:04.620 --> 46:05.380
not equivalent

46:05.380 --> 46:06.040
if they took

46:06.040 --> 46:07.240
different historical paths

46:07.240 --> 46:07.780
to get there.

46:08.060 --> 46:09.300
Let's really emphasize this.

46:09.640 --> 46:10.560
If I write code

46:10.560 --> 46:11.480
that takes path A

46:11.480 --> 46:12.840
to output the number 5,

46:13.200 --> 46:13.880
and the compiler

46:13.880 --> 46:14.860
thinks it's being clever

46:14.860 --> 46:16.520
and optimizes the execution

46:16.520 --> 46:17.860
to take a faster path B

46:17.860 --> 46:19.100
to output the number 5,

46:19.460 --> 46:20.420
the compiler is broken.

46:20.740 --> 46:21.840
The compiler is broken

46:21.840 --> 46:23.280
because it altered the history.

46:24.040 --> 46:24.380
Remember,

46:24.820 --> 46:25.480
in sphere-pop,

46:25.720 --> 46:27.300
types are historical certificates.

46:27.960 --> 46:29.320
If the compiler silently

46:29.320 --> 46:30.380
changes path A

46:30.380 --> 46:31.120
to path B,

46:31.580 --> 46:32.540
the compiled code

46:32.540 --> 46:33.040
might output

46:33.040 --> 46:34.260
the correct number today,

46:34.520 --> 46:36.200
but it might fatally fail

46:36.200 --> 46:37.380
a type check tomorrow

46:37.380 --> 46:38.720
when a future operation

46:38.720 --> 46:40.260
relies on the specific,

46:40.720 --> 46:41.560
certified events

46:41.560 --> 46:42.140
that were supposed

46:42.140 --> 46:43.400
to happen on path A.

46:43.400 --> 46:44.160
The path

46:44.160 --> 46:45.060
is the program.

46:45.880 --> 46:46.700
Output equivalence

46:46.700 --> 46:47.460
is a strictly

46:47.460 --> 46:48.620
weaker criterion

46:48.620 --> 46:49.820
that is completely

46:49.820 --> 46:51.080
insufficient for a framework

46:51.080 --> 46:51.760
where history

46:51.760 --> 46:53.220
is the primary substance.

46:53.440 --> 46:53.560
Okay,

46:53.660 --> 46:54.140
we have to wade

46:54.140 --> 46:55.480
into the really deep math now.

46:55.640 --> 46:56.180
Section 8,

46:56.640 --> 46:57.340
the deep math,

46:57.620 --> 46:58.760
calculus of constructions,

46:58.900 --> 46:59.600
and BNF.

47:00.000 --> 47:00.720
To mathematically

47:00.720 --> 47:01.940
bind this entire

47:01.940 --> 47:02.800
universe together,

47:03.180 --> 47:04.220
the types of certificates,

47:04.420 --> 47:05.140
the incident history,

47:05.500 --> 47:06.800
the precise collapse rules,

47:06.920 --> 47:08.240
the careful garbage collection,

47:08.740 --> 47:09.900
Flyxion couldn't just use

47:09.900 --> 47:11.220
standard algebraic logic.

47:11.220 --> 47:12.120
They had to adopt

47:12.120 --> 47:13.800
specific foundational systems,

47:14.060 --> 47:15.460
specifically something called

47:15.460 --> 47:17.000
the calculus of constructions,

47:17.180 --> 47:17.720
or COSI.

47:18.120 --> 47:19.140
We touched on the edges

47:19.140 --> 47:19.920
of this earlier

47:19.920 --> 47:20.860
when you asked

47:20.860 --> 47:22.600
why simple type systems fail.

47:23.600 --> 47:24.900
The calculus of constructions

47:24.900 --> 47:26.120
is a highly advanced

47:26.120 --> 47:27.140
formal system

47:27.140 --> 47:28.020
in pipe theory

47:28.020 --> 47:29.200
that allows for

47:29.200 --> 47:30.020
dependent types.

47:30.420 --> 47:31.420
It allows types

47:31.420 --> 47:32.900
to dynamically depend

47:32.900 --> 47:34.460
on runtime values.

47:34.920 --> 47:35.400
I want to use

47:35.400 --> 47:36.600
that file reading example

47:36.600 --> 47:37.760
again to make this concrete.

47:37.920 --> 47:38.160
Perfect.

47:38.400 --> 47:39.260
If you write a function

47:39.260 --> 47:40.460
to read a file in Java,

47:40.460 --> 47:42.060
a simple type system

47:42.060 --> 47:42.920
says the output

47:42.920 --> 47:43.480
of that function

47:43.480 --> 47:44.100
is a string.

47:44.480 --> 47:45.080
It just tells you

47:45.080 --> 47:45.860
the shape of the data.

47:46.260 --> 47:47.200
The compiler doesn't know

47:47.200 --> 47:48.280
which specific file

47:48.280 --> 47:49.400
produced the string.

47:49.740 --> 47:50.340
In the calculus

47:50.340 --> 47:51.340
of constructions,

47:51.620 --> 47:52.460
the type of the output

47:52.460 --> 47:53.560
can actually encapsulate

47:53.560 --> 47:55.040
the specific runtime value

47:55.040 --> 47:55.880
that generated it.

47:56.420 --> 47:57.620
The type isn't just string.

47:58.000 --> 47:59.340
The type is file contents

47:59.340 --> 48:00.900
with a specific path attached.

48:01.560 --> 48:03.060
The type dynamically morphs

48:03.060 --> 48:03.980
depending on the specific

48:03.980 --> 48:05.140
file path you feed it.

48:05.260 --> 48:06.660
That feels incredibly powerful.

48:07.060 --> 48:07.780
The type system

48:07.780 --> 48:09.480
is aware of the actual data

48:09.480 --> 48:10.600
flowing through the program.

48:11.000 --> 48:11.360
It is.

48:11.640 --> 48:11.940
Furthermore,

48:12.300 --> 48:13.280
Kurian allows terms

48:13.280 --> 48:14.340
to function as types,

48:14.820 --> 48:15.560
blurring the line

48:15.560 --> 48:16.780
between data and structure.

48:17.420 --> 48:18.580
To prevent the mathematics

48:18.580 --> 48:19.300
from collapsing

48:19.300 --> 48:20.360
into paradoxes,

48:20.560 --> 48:21.440
like Russell's paradox,

48:22.000 --> 48:23.160
where a set contains

48:23.160 --> 48:24.200
all sets that don't

48:24.200 --> 48:25.000
contain themselves,

48:25.500 --> 48:26.200
Kurki employs

48:26.200 --> 48:28.020
a strict universe hierarchy.

48:28.260 --> 48:29.120
I always think of

48:29.120 --> 48:30.080
Russell's paradox

48:30.080 --> 48:31.200
as a snake eating

48:31.200 --> 48:32.000
its own tail

48:32.000 --> 48:33.360
until the universe implodes.

48:33.460 --> 48:33.800
Exactly.

48:34.220 --> 48:35.060
To stop the snake

48:35.060 --> 48:35.980
from eating itself,

48:36.080 --> 48:37.300
Kurki separates logic

48:37.300 --> 48:37.980
into layers.

48:38.560 --> 48:40.340
Type 0, type 1, type 2

48:40.340 --> 48:41.600
ascending infinitely.

48:42.120 --> 48:42.880
A type at level 1

48:42.880 --> 48:44.000
can only describe things

48:44.000 --> 48:44.580
at level 0.

48:45.160 --> 48:45.820
This hierarchy

48:45.820 --> 48:46.860
acts as a mathematical

48:46.860 --> 48:48.020
scaffolding to ensure

48:48.020 --> 48:49.240
the system remains sound

48:49.240 --> 48:50.620
and doesn't swallow itself

48:50.620 --> 48:51.520
in a recursive loop

48:51.520 --> 48:52.160
of definitions.

48:52.600 --> 48:53.000
But then,

48:53.100 --> 48:54.040
right after diving

48:54.040 --> 48:55.640
into this massive type theory,

48:55.840 --> 48:56.460
the author goes on

48:56.460 --> 48:57.160
this huge tangent

48:57.160 --> 48:58.200
about BNF grammar.

48:58.400 --> 48:59.320
Back is now form.

48:59.600 --> 49:00.940
It feels almost out of place.

49:01.240 --> 49:02.620
He compares BNF notation

49:02.620 --> 49:04.040
to physical circuit diagrams

49:04.040 --> 49:05.440
and standard programming code

49:05.440 --> 49:06.600
and claims BNF

49:06.600 --> 49:07.640
is vastly superior.

49:08.040 --> 49:09.300
Why does syntax notation

49:09.300 --> 49:10.120
matter this much

49:10.120 --> 49:10.720
to the architecture?

49:11.000 --> 49:12.280
It circles back beautifully

49:12.280 --> 49:13.400
to the note taxonomy

49:13.400 --> 49:14.280
we discussed earlier.

49:14.800 --> 49:15.720
Freckney views BNF

49:15.720 --> 49:16.620
not just as syntax

49:16.620 --> 49:17.840
but as the ultimate

49:17.840 --> 49:19.140
generative refusal note.

49:19.380 --> 49:20.240
How does grammar

49:20.240 --> 49:21.460
act as a refusal note?

49:21.900 --> 49:22.740
A BNF grammar

49:22.740 --> 49:23.920
is a set of rules

49:23.920 --> 49:24.760
that specifies

49:24.760 --> 49:26.040
exactly what strings

49:26.040 --> 49:26.560
of characters

49:26.560 --> 49:27.880
are mathematically valid

49:27.880 --> 49:28.800
in a given language

49:28.800 --> 49:30.100
and by definition,

49:30.620 --> 49:31.920
it rigorously refuses

49:31.920 --> 49:33.200
all other combinations.

49:33.520 --> 49:34.960
It is pure specification.

49:34.960 --> 49:36.640
A physical circuit diagram

49:36.640 --> 49:37.800
is severely limited

49:37.800 --> 49:38.900
because it is

49:38.900 --> 49:40.180
inextricably tied

49:40.180 --> 49:41.480
to the physical topology

49:41.480 --> 49:42.580
of copper wires

49:42.580 --> 49:43.520
and silicon gates.

49:43.780 --> 49:44.520
A C program

49:44.520 --> 49:45.800
is tied to the quirks

49:45.800 --> 49:47.020
of a specific compiler

49:47.020 --> 49:47.580
architecture

49:47.580 --> 49:49.380
and the CPU instruction set

49:49.380 --> 49:51.040
but BNF operates

49:51.040 --> 49:52.280
at a level of abstraction

49:52.280 --> 49:53.460
completely unmoored

49:53.460 --> 49:54.060
from hardware.

49:54.260 --> 49:55.560
It's the pure idea

49:55.560 --> 49:56.120
of the language.

49:56.220 --> 49:56.540
Yes.

49:56.880 --> 49:57.820
It is human readable,

49:58.240 --> 49:59.380
it is machine parsable,

49:59.660 --> 50:00.400
and it serves

50:00.400 --> 50:01.940
as a mathematical firewall

50:01.940 --> 50:03.580
against implementation bias.

50:03.580 --> 50:04.740
When you write

50:04.740 --> 50:05.560
a system's rules

50:05.560 --> 50:06.120
in BNF,

50:06.280 --> 50:06.820
you are creating

50:06.820 --> 50:07.740
a generative artifact

50:07.740 --> 50:08.640
that opens up

50:08.640 --> 50:09.380
new possibilities,

50:09.880 --> 50:10.460
you can generate

50:10.460 --> 50:11.840
infinite valid programs

50:11.840 --> 50:13.000
while simultaneously

50:13.000 --> 50:14.620
erecting an impenetrable

50:14.620 --> 50:15.580
wall of refusal

50:15.580 --> 50:17.180
against invalid structures.

50:17.520 --> 50:18.240
And as if that

50:18.240 --> 50:19.080
wasn't deep enough,

50:19.380 --> 50:20.080
the source drops

50:20.080 --> 50:21.440
some incredibly dense

50:21.440 --> 50:22.600
highlights from part 7

50:22.600 --> 50:23.300
of the monograph,

50:23.860 --> 50:24.800
specifically pointing

50:24.800 --> 50:25.560
to unifications

50:25.560 --> 50:26.900
involving sheaf semantics

50:26.900 --> 50:28.760
and active geodesic inference.

50:29.140 --> 50:29.660
I want to focus

50:29.660 --> 50:30.280
on that last one.

50:30.740 --> 50:31.520
What in the world

50:31.520 --> 50:33.400
is active geodesic inference?

50:33.880 --> 50:34.680
This is the moment

50:34.680 --> 50:35.220
in the paper

50:35.220 --> 50:36.000
where affliction

50:36.000 --> 50:37.440
stretches the architecture

50:37.440 --> 50:38.880
beyond computer science

50:38.880 --> 50:40.940
and into theoretical physics

50:40.940 --> 50:42.240
and artificial intelligence.

50:42.840 --> 50:43.860
The author proposes

50:43.860 --> 50:44.920
a new definition

50:44.920 --> 50:46.400
of intelligence itself.

50:46.960 --> 50:47.840
In this framework,

50:48.040 --> 50:49.460
intelligence is not defined

50:49.460 --> 50:50.120
as the ability

50:50.120 --> 50:51.960
to compute the correct answer

50:51.960 --> 50:53.400
to a static problem.

50:54.020 --> 50:55.060
Intelligence is defined

50:55.060 --> 50:55.980
as the capacity

50:55.980 --> 50:57.200
of an active system

50:57.200 --> 50:58.140
to maintain

50:58.140 --> 50:59.700
low action trajectories

50:59.700 --> 51:01.260
through possibility space.

51:01.260 --> 51:02.440
Let's unpack that carefully.

51:02.620 --> 51:03.940
Low action trajectories.

51:04.320 --> 51:05.140
Imagine a hiker

51:05.140 --> 51:06.060
attempting to cross

51:06.060 --> 51:07.320
a massive treacherous

51:07.320 --> 51:08.060
mountain range.

51:08.520 --> 51:09.620
An unintelligent system

51:09.620 --> 51:10.500
just calculates

51:10.500 --> 51:11.640
a straight line vector

51:11.640 --> 51:12.480
to the destination

51:12.480 --> 51:14.000
and starts walking

51:14.000 --> 51:14.580
mindlessly,

51:14.780 --> 51:16.020
expending massive amounts

51:16.020 --> 51:16.500
of energy,

51:16.700 --> 51:18.200
climbing over sheer cliffs

51:18.200 --> 51:19.340
and wading through

51:19.340 --> 51:20.100
deep swamps.

51:20.420 --> 51:21.180
It has a goal

51:21.180 --> 51:22.020
but no awareness

51:22.020 --> 51:22.620
of the terrain.

51:22.680 --> 51:23.200
Exactly.

51:23.620 --> 51:24.680
An intelligent system,

51:24.800 --> 51:25.660
the experienced hiker,

51:25.840 --> 51:27.200
actively reads the terrain.

51:27.500 --> 51:28.420
They look for the passes,

51:28.700 --> 51:29.680
they follow the contours

51:29.680 --> 51:30.280
of the valleys,

51:30.280 --> 51:31.040
they avoid the

51:31.040 --> 51:32.060
high friction obstacles,

51:32.060 --> 51:33.320
they find the path

51:33.320 --> 51:34.280
of least resistance.

51:34.820 --> 51:35.280
In physics,

51:35.840 --> 51:37.240
this path of least resistance

51:37.240 --> 51:38.500
through a curved space

51:38.500 --> 51:39.880
is called a geodesic.

51:40.120 --> 51:41.760
So active geodesic inference

51:41.760 --> 51:43.420
means an intelligent system

51:43.420 --> 51:44.700
is actively predicting

51:44.700 --> 51:45.340
and shaping

51:45.340 --> 51:46.320
its own future

51:46.320 --> 51:47.660
configuration space.

51:47.900 --> 51:48.940
It uses notes,

51:49.080 --> 51:49.980
it uses memory,

51:50.400 --> 51:51.840
it uses the refuse operator

51:51.840 --> 51:53.180
to document the cliffs

51:53.180 --> 51:53.760
and swamps,

51:54.200 --> 51:55.520
specifically to avoid

51:55.520 --> 51:56.340
high cost,

51:56.580 --> 51:57.600
inadmissible paths.

51:57.600 --> 51:59.280
It stays on a safe,

51:59.720 --> 52:00.720
low energy trajectory

52:00.720 --> 52:02.320
through the absolute chaos

52:02.320 --> 52:03.760
of infinite possibility.

52:04.160 --> 52:04.460
Wow.

52:04.700 --> 52:06.300
That completely redefines

52:06.300 --> 52:07.060
what learning is.

52:07.360 --> 52:09.000
Learning isn't memorizing facts.

52:09.220 --> 52:09.920
Learning is building

52:09.920 --> 52:11.020
a map of refusal

52:11.020 --> 52:12.000
so you stop walking

52:12.000 --> 52:12.680
into walls.

52:13.160 --> 52:13.760
And that brings

52:13.760 --> 52:14.980
this entire deep dive

52:14.980 --> 52:16.140
to its final sweeping

52:16.140 --> 52:17.560
conclusion in section nine.

52:18.020 --> 52:19.040
Civilization is note

52:19.040 --> 52:19.680
infrastructure.

52:19.980 --> 52:21.140
We zoom out from code,

52:21.500 --> 52:22.580
from category theory,

52:22.800 --> 52:23.940
from artificial intelligence,

52:23.940 --> 52:25.040
all the way to the

52:25.040 --> 52:26.060
macroscopic scale

52:26.060 --> 52:26.820
of human history.

52:26.820 --> 52:27.920
The author introduces

52:27.920 --> 52:28.840
a concept termed

52:28.840 --> 52:30.620
the MEM-8 cognitive substrate.

52:31.520 --> 52:32.360
It is the culminating

52:32.360 --> 52:33.400
thesis of the monograph.

52:34.100 --> 52:35.420
It posits that the human brain

52:35.420 --> 52:36.460
at its biological core

52:36.460 --> 52:37.340
is not a database.

52:37.560 --> 52:38.200
It is not a warehouse

52:38.200 --> 52:39.320
with boxes on shelves.

52:39.440 --> 52:40.680
The brain is an event structure.

52:40.980 --> 52:42.420
So a spiral-bound notebook,

52:42.840 --> 52:44.180
a digital Git repository,

52:44.880 --> 52:46.400
and human biological memory

52:46.400 --> 52:48.240
are all just miniature instances

52:48.240 --> 52:49.480
of the exact same

52:49.480 --> 52:51.160
trajectory-preserving architecture.

52:51.420 --> 52:52.620
They are all substrates

52:52.620 --> 52:54.300
designed to capture continuations.

52:54.940 --> 52:55.700
The brain stores

52:55.700 --> 52:56.860
infrastructure trajectories,

52:57.180 --> 52:58.740
sequences of sensory events,

52:59.140 --> 53:00.180
emotions, decisions,

53:00.780 --> 53:01.800
organized specifically

53:01.800 --> 53:02.600
to make future

53:02.600 --> 53:03.860
reconstruction possible.

53:04.180 --> 53:05.500
And this unified theory

53:05.500 --> 53:07.220
leads to the most chilling part

53:07.220 --> 53:08.580
of the entire monograph,

53:08.940 --> 53:10.740
theorem 21.3,

53:11.120 --> 53:12.220
which Flyxion calls

53:12.220 --> 53:13.740
the Civilizational Collapse

53:13.740 --> 53:14.260
theorem.

53:14.480 --> 53:15.880
I read the theorem three times.

53:16.380 --> 53:17.420
What does it actually state

53:17.420 --> 53:19.160
about the fragility of society?

53:19.480 --> 53:20.180
The theorem states

53:20.180 --> 53:20.880
that a civilization

53:20.880 --> 53:22.200
that loses its underlying

53:22.200 --> 53:23.080
node infrastructure

53:23.080 --> 53:24.260
does not automatically

53:24.260 --> 53:25.040
become ignorant.

53:25.620 --> 53:26.380
It does not necessarily

53:26.380 --> 53:27.940
lose its accumulated facts

53:27.940 --> 53:28.520
or its data.

53:28.920 --> 53:30.320
It simply loses its ability

53:30.320 --> 53:31.440
to reach its own knowledge.

53:31.660 --> 53:32.580
That is terrifying.

53:32.760 --> 53:34.340
It's the library analogy again,

53:34.440 --> 53:35.680
but on a planetary scale.

53:36.180 --> 53:37.020
The scientific sacks

53:37.020 --> 53:37.560
are still there.

53:37.640 --> 53:38.280
The books are still

53:38.280 --> 53:39.100
sitting on the shelves.

53:39.240 --> 53:40.500
But the Civilizational

53:40.500 --> 53:41.200
card catalog

53:41.200 --> 53:42.340
has burned to ash.

53:42.340 --> 53:43.840
The underlying truths

53:43.840 --> 53:44.900
of physics, art,

53:45.000 --> 53:45.440
and medicine

53:45.440 --> 53:47.160
remain completely unchanged,

53:47.640 --> 53:48.440
but the collective

53:48.440 --> 53:49.680
reachability plummets.

53:50.480 --> 53:51.920
The cost of reconstructing

53:51.920 --> 53:52.340
the knowledge

53:52.340 --> 53:53.420
from first principles

53:53.420 --> 53:55.680
becomes so impossibly high

53:55.680 --> 53:57.060
that the civilization

53:57.060 --> 53:58.240
effectively collapses,

53:58.800 --> 53:59.820
even while surrounded

53:59.820 --> 54:01.500
by its own unreadable data.

54:02.120 --> 54:03.280
When you look at the progression

54:03.280 --> 54:04.380
of human technology

54:04.380 --> 54:05.120
through the lens

54:05.120 --> 54:05.740
of this theorem,

54:06.280 --> 54:06.980
everything makes

54:06.980 --> 54:08.460
a terrifying amount of sense.

54:08.760 --> 54:10.780
The evolution from oral speech

54:10.780 --> 54:12.220
to written language

54:12.220 --> 54:13.460
to the printing press

54:13.460 --> 54:14.100
to the internet

54:14.100 --> 54:15.760
to digital version control.

54:16.380 --> 54:17.280
It isn't just a story

54:17.280 --> 54:18.560
of humanity inventing ways

54:18.560 --> 54:19.620
to communicate faster.

54:20.060 --> 54:21.400
It is a story of humanity

54:21.400 --> 54:22.600
desperately expanding

54:22.600 --> 54:23.900
its reachability horizon

54:23.900 --> 54:25.840
to prevent trajectory failure.

54:26.700 --> 54:27.320
Oral storytelling

54:27.320 --> 54:28.060
prevents the deaths

54:28.060 --> 54:28.860
of a single witness.

54:29.400 --> 54:30.000
The printing press

54:30.000 --> 54:30.800
prevents the destruction

54:30.800 --> 54:32.080
of an individual manuscript.

54:32.760 --> 54:33.980
Git prevents the loss

54:33.980 --> 54:34.920
of a software trajectory.

54:35.520 --> 54:36.260
We are just building

54:36.260 --> 54:37.240
larger and larger

54:37.240 --> 54:38.560
anti-collapse artifacts,

54:39.140 --> 54:39.960
trying to stay ahead

54:39.960 --> 54:40.520
of the entropy

54:40.520 --> 54:41.400
that wants to erase

54:41.400 --> 54:41.940
our history.

54:42.320 --> 54:43.360
We are continually

54:43.360 --> 54:44.620
constructing scaffolding

54:44.620 --> 54:45.260
to guarantee

54:45.260 --> 54:46.800
our own future reachability.

54:47.540 --> 54:48.480
The note infrastructure

54:48.480 --> 54:49.760
is the only thing

54:49.760 --> 54:50.400
standing between

54:50.400 --> 54:51.600
a functioning society

54:51.600 --> 54:53.880
and the abyss of amnesia.

54:54.140 --> 54:55.100
This has been an actually

54:55.100 --> 54:56.500
massive intellectual journey.

54:56.980 --> 54:58.040
We started by realizing

54:58.040 --> 54:58.780
that your simple

54:58.780 --> 54:59.920
crumpled grocery list

54:59.920 --> 55:02.020
is actually a time machine

55:02.020 --> 55:03.240
projecting into the future.

55:03.240 --> 55:04.220
We walked through

55:04.220 --> 55:05.400
the mathematical proofs

55:05.400 --> 55:06.260
showing that our comforting

55:06.260 --> 55:07.060
present state

55:07.060 --> 55:08.360
is just an illusion

55:08.360 --> 55:09.700
hiding a vast ocean

55:09.700 --> 55:10.320
of history.

55:10.520 --> 55:11.900
We explored the mechanics

55:11.900 --> 55:13.280
of building a computer language

55:13.280 --> 55:14.400
purely out of choices

55:14.400 --> 55:15.160
and refusals,

55:15.560 --> 55:16.520
completely abandoning

55:16.520 --> 55:17.680
the concept of a variable.

55:18.160 --> 55:19.180
And we redefined

55:19.180 --> 55:20.500
both artificial intelligence

55:20.500 --> 55:22.320
and civilizational memory

55:22.320 --> 55:23.300
in the process.

55:23.440 --> 55:24.120
The convergence

55:24.120 --> 55:25.060
the author promised

55:25.060 --> 55:25.620
at the beginning

55:25.620 --> 55:26.260
holds true.

55:27.040 --> 55:27.940
A note is an

55:27.940 --> 55:29.160
externalized continuation.

55:29.880 --> 55:30.940
A history is an

55:30.940 --> 55:31.940
internalized note.

55:32.480 --> 55:33.040
The boundaries

55:33.040 --> 55:34.260
between human cognition

55:34.260 --> 55:35.720
and computational architecture

55:35.720 --> 55:36.280
dissolve.

55:36.760 --> 55:37.340
Which brings me

55:37.340 --> 55:38.160
to a final thought

55:38.160 --> 55:38.480
for you,

55:38.540 --> 55:38.920
the listener.

55:39.700 --> 55:40.400
As we wrap up

55:40.400 --> 55:41.180
this exploration,

55:41.640 --> 55:42.300
I want you to think

55:42.300 --> 55:43.040
about the digital

55:43.040 --> 55:44.160
hoards on your phone

55:44.160 --> 55:44.740
right now.

55:45.380 --> 55:45.900
Think about your

55:45.900 --> 55:46.620
browser window

55:46.620 --> 55:48.220
with 80 open tabs.

55:48.920 --> 55:49.420
Think about your

55:49.420 --> 55:50.380
Read It Later app

55:50.380 --> 55:51.680
overflowing with articles

55:51.680 --> 55:53.160
you've saved for three years.

55:53.820 --> 55:54.460
Think about your

55:54.460 --> 55:55.200
unstructured,

55:55.460 --> 55:56.400
messy, chaotic

55:56.400 --> 55:57.200
notes app.

55:57.420 --> 55:58.120
Ask yourself,

55:58.240 --> 55:58.940
are you actually

55:58.940 --> 55:59.800
building true

55:59.800 --> 56:01.160
anti-collapse artifacts

56:01.160 --> 56:02.460
that are mathematically

56:02.460 --> 56:03.680
guaranteed to secure

56:03.680 --> 56:04.600
your future reachability?

56:04.600 --> 56:05.760
Or are you just

56:05.760 --> 56:06.940
hoarding degenerate state?

56:07.280 --> 56:07.780
If a system's

56:07.780 --> 56:08.380
true intelligence

56:08.380 --> 56:09.180
is defined by

56:09.180 --> 56:09.920
its ability to

56:09.920 --> 56:10.840
maintain a low

56:10.840 --> 56:11.740
action trajectory

56:11.740 --> 56:13.120
through possibility space,

56:13.420 --> 56:14.520
how intelligent is

56:14.520 --> 56:15.340
your personal note

56:15.340 --> 56:16.500
infrastructure right now?

56:16.860 --> 56:17.520
It is a question

56:17.520 --> 56:18.280
that demands a

56:18.280 --> 56:19.540
hard, honest look

56:19.540 --> 56:20.260
at what we are

56:20.260 --> 56:21.160
actually preserving

56:21.160 --> 56:21.900
and what we are

56:21.900 --> 56:23.060
inadvertently allowing

56:23.060 --> 56:23.720
to collapse.

56:24.000 --> 56:24.780
We started today

56:24.780 --> 56:25.380
by talking about

56:25.380 --> 56:26.440
that comforting illusion

56:26.440 --> 56:27.380
of the organized

56:27.380 --> 56:27.800
warehouse.

56:28.300 --> 56:29.140
We like the idea

56:29.140 --> 56:29.880
that our memories,

56:30.260 --> 56:30.680
our data,

56:30.820 --> 56:31.400
and our lives

56:31.400 --> 56:32.460
are just neat

56:32.460 --> 56:33.060
little boxes

56:33.060 --> 56:33.960
sitting statically

56:33.960 --> 56:34.540
on a shelf.

56:35.180 --> 56:35.780
But as the

56:35.780 --> 56:36.700
sphere pop architecture

56:36.700 --> 56:37.820
shows us so visibly,

56:38.200 --> 56:38.880
there is no shelf.

56:39.460 --> 56:40.680
There is no static present.

56:41.320 --> 56:42.000
There are only

56:42.000 --> 56:43.100
the historical paths

56:43.100 --> 56:44.000
we took to get here

56:44.000 --> 56:45.000
and the fragile

56:45.000 --> 56:46.020
continuations we are

56:46.020 --> 56:46.780
desperately trying

56:46.780 --> 56:47.340
to keep open

56:47.340 --> 56:47.940
for tomorrow.

56:48.600 --> 56:49.200
Keep exploring

56:49.200 --> 56:50.180
those deep structures

56:50.180 --> 56:51.320
beneath everyday life.

56:51.740 --> 56:52.500
We'll see you next time.

